\subsection{Overview}

Across the literature on agricultural subsurface (tile) drainage, authors converge on a shared meta-conclusion: despite decades of field and modelling work, the interactions among crop yield, nitrogen (N) and phosphorus (P) losses, greenhouse-gas emissions, and the field water balance remain insufficiently characterised across the full range of soils, climates, cropping systems, and drainage-management practices that occur in tile-drained agriculture. Individual practices and processes are increasingly well described, yet the field still lacks the long-term, multi-scale, and economically grounded evidence needed to design, target, and deploy cost-effective nutrient-mitigation strategies at the landscape scale. \textcite{Ma2025} distil this programme broadly, arguing that far more research is needed to understand comprehensively the impacts of conservation practices on productivity, nutrient-loss reduction, ecosystem services, and their tradeoffs in tile-drained ecosystems.

This section frames the research frontier around what remains uncertain or unstudied rather than around settled results. Several recurring lines of inquiry structure the synthesis that follows. First, there is a near-universal call for longer-duration, multi-year, multi-site monitoring to smooth climatic variability and capture the true magnitude and persistence of system responses \parencite{Randall2003,Randall2005,Waring2024,Jeong2018,Cooke2012,Kokulan2019,RANDALL1995}. Second, N management---rate, timing, form, placement, and crediting of residual and mineralisable N---remains the primary but incompletely optimised lever, together with the temporal targeting of losses \parencite{Christianson2015b,Bandura2025,Liu2021,ROSSI1991,Zhao2000}. Third, P transport in subsurface drainage is repeatedly flagged as under-studied relative to its importance, particularly for dissolved, colloidal, and bioavailable fractions and for the metrics used to predict loss \parencite{Hertzberger2019,Yuan2022,deOliveira2022,Sharpley1995,Christianson2016}. Fourth, no single best management practice (BMP) is a ``silver bullet'': bundling in-field, edge-of-field, and water-management practices and targeting them to hydrologically active critical source areas is essential \parencite{Sunohara2016,Lenhart2016,Christianson2014,Baulch2019}. Fifth, process-based models are expected to carry much of the predictive burden, yet substantial algorithmic gaps remain---especially for winter, soil carbon--nitrogen (C--N) cycling, macropore flow, and groundwater coupling \parencite{Du2017,SKAGGS1994,Dietzel2016,Feyereisen2006b}. Finally, cutting across all of these, authors stress that biophysical understanding must be coupled with economics, incentive design, policy reform, remote sensing and decision-support tools, and stakeholder engagement before mitigation can be scaled \parencite{Liu2021,Kvítek2023,David2015,Ferin2023,Khanal2020}. The subsections below organise these open questions and priorities in turn, retaining the specific quantitative targets, methods, and conditions proposed by the source studies.

\subsection{Long-term, multi-scale monitoring and experimental design}

A dominant frontier priority is the need for longer observation periods to disentangle management effects from climatic noise. \textcite{Randall2003,Randall2005} argue that long-term studies with detailed measurements are necessary to smooth year-to-year climatic variability, and stress that both the corn and soybean phases of a rotation should be evaluated when assessing N-management effects on nitrate losses. \textcite{Waring2024} similarly conclude that long-term studies are essential to capture a system's response to conservation practices and the true magnitude of that response, emphasising site-specificity, while \textcite{Cooke2012} note that studies of longer duration and many more years of yield data are required to determine drainage water management (DWM) effects on yield free of confounding factors. \textcite{Pittelkow2017} likewise stress evaluating N losses over multiple years on an annualised basis, and continuous monitoring of N application rate, timing, and method on nitrate loss and yield is advocated to give modelling a more rational setup \parencite{Jeong2018}. \textcite{RANDALL1995} add that only long-term studies across a range of climatic conditions can yield appropriate guidelines, and that plant, soil, and water measurements must be gathered together to uncover interactions among crop production, climate, and environmental impact.

Several studies extend this call to non-growing-season and multi-year hydrologic coverage. \textcite{VanEsbroeck2016} recommend collecting field data during the non-growing season when estimating P loads and BMP efficacy, and call for data across a wider range of soil textures, management practices, and hydrologic years---including wetter summers---before drawing conclusions on optimum P-application timing. \textcite{vanderSalm2012} argue that data spanning at least a normal, a wet, and a dry year are necessary to understand losses and transport routes, and \textcite{Ulén2007} note that as long a record as possible is needed for trend estimation because water-quality change may lag the P-loss change driven by agricultural measures. Long-term or continued monitoring is repeatedly invoked to evaluate P leaching and nutrient removal of infiltration systems \parencite{Duan2015}, to determine whether enhanced effluent-driven P losses persist \parencite{Monaghan2004}, to confirm bioreactor and saturated-buffer effectiveness across soils, landscapes, and weather years \parencite{Jaynes2019}, and to obtain definitive evidence of suspended-solids and orthophosphate reductions under cover cropping \parencite{SalcedaGonzalez2025}. Long-term designs are also invoked to detect slow or subtle change---in soil organic carbon recovery under rotation shifts \parencite{Kumar2014}, in discharge-induced organic-matter export \parencite{Manninen2023}, and in whether drainage-induced soil evolution continues or stabilises over decades \parencite{Frison2009}. Several teams describe active or planned long-term programmes in rice--wheat and rice--oil rotations \parencite{Qi2025b}, in subsurface pipe and seepage drainage across diverse environments \parencite{Liu2025b,Liu2025c}, and note that four years of data are insufficient to resolve residue-cover effects on pollutant losses and yield \parencite{Thoma2005,Acharya2019b}.

Experimental-design refinements are proposed alongside longer records. \textcite{Schilling2013} recommend establishing a suitable correlation among paired basins across a range of climates, restricting paired sampling to the March--July high-flow period, delaying post-BMP monitoring to enhance the likelihood of detecting change, and using BMPs capable of achieving at least the minimum detectable change. \textcite{Sunohara2016} argue that BMP impacts on downstream receptors should be assessed simultaneously at multiple scales, from field to watershed, and \textcite{Irvine2019} call for within-field and within-plot studies to elucidate mechanistic processes of nutrient export and for additional field-scale studies within high-export sub-watersheds. Further experiments under wider conditions are sought to test effective-yield formulas and models \parencite{Xin2016c}, refined $^{15}$N loss observation over larger, more representative trial areas is advocated \parencite{Xiong2025}, and mesocosms are seen as an opportunity to quantify nutrient-loss responses to management change \parencite{Loper2024}. Underlying these appeals is the imperative that longer records are only useful if sampling is standardised: soil biological and soil-health indicators should be sampled at consistent times under similar moisture and temperature conditions \parencite{Sherbine2023,Li2023b}, and meta-analytic comparability requires complete hydrologic description of each plot, standardised analytical methods, adequate monitoring periods, and full reporting of concentrations with descriptive statistics \parencite{Ghirardini2019}.

\subsection{Nitrogen management: rates, timing, form, crediting, and loss timing}

Refining fertiliser N management is the most consistently identified priority. \textcite{ROSSI1991} state the urgency plainly: management directed at substantial nitrate reductions is needed, with careful evaluation of the effects of fertiliser timing, rate, form, and placement. \textcite{Zhao2000} argue efforts should concentrate on strategies that increase N-use efficiency in corn---optimal rate, timing, and form---while \textcite{Randall2001} call to apply the correct rate at the optimum time, credit previous legumes and manure, and improve manure management through known nutrient content, uniform spreading, and timely incorporation. At watershed scale, \textcite{Dattamudi2020} conclude that N-application schemes need reevaluation, suggesting lower corn rates, precision agriculture, use of soil N with high-yielding varieties, and split, properly timed applications; \textcite{Elmi2002} likewise see reductions from matching rate and timing to crop uptake. Optimising N rates is expected to continue receiving primary research and regulatory focus, and accurate 4R implementation will require site-specific knowledge to balance agronomic and environmental goals \parencite{Christianson2015b}.

\subsubsection{Next-generation rate tools and decision support}

\textcite{Bandura2025} propose concrete standards for the next generation of N-rate tools: they should be developed and refined for various application timings using the economically optimum N rate (EONR) as a benchmark, judged adequate if they predict N need within 10~kg~N~ha$^{-1}$ of EONR, and environmentally unacceptable if they exceed EONR at an N:corn price ratio of 5.6 by more than 30~kg~N~ha$^{-1}$. \textcite{Liu2021} call for more accurate recommendations that weigh environmental and economic benefits, effective local nutrient plans, case-by-case cost analyses, and coordinated agency action (e.g.\ USDA-NRCS with USEPA) to promote 4R adoption. \textcite{Banger2018} advocate additional experiments across wider management and soil types to identify potential yield tradeoffs from rate reductions, noting that if yield-loss risks appear, emphasis should shift toward cover crops and edge-of-field practices to meet load-reduction targets. Comprehensive tools integrating reliable weather forecasts, historical management, local field and soil parameters, and crop-variety information are proposed to estimate optimum rates, alongside regional long-term agronomic-optimum-N-rate (AONR) means and study of the planting-date/efficiency/AONR relationship \parencite{Maas2022}, and precision application based on soil-N availability \parencite{Gupta2023}. \textcite{Dinnes2002} prioritise better crediting of non-fertiliser N sources, improved in-season soil-N monitoring for split applications, and reliable variable-rate models incorporating remote sensing and hyperspectral approaches, while \textcite{Cassman2025} propose low-cost proxy variables---yield without applied N as a field-scale estimate of soil N-supply capacity, and partial factor productivity of applied N and rainfall---for monitoring soil N and water supply.

Local-scale testing and decision approaches require refinement. Late-spring nitrate-test (LSNT) programmes are framed as an initial start to be replaced or refined as new research emerges, with high-clearance equipment to reduce delayed-sidedress risk and cropping diversity or edge-of-field denitrification needed to reach drinking-water goals \parencite{Jaynes2004}; grain-quality and grain-protein monitoring plus risk insurance are recommended for LSNT adopters \parencite{Karlen2005}; and weather-based model refinement into simple tools, with improved understanding of year-to-year soil mineral-N variability, is proposed to reduce soil-testing needs and increase side-dressing adoption \parencite{Malone2010}. \textcite{Jaynes2013} call for research to pinpoint where between the V12 and V16 growth stages sidedress N ceases to be beneficial, and \textcite{Jaynes2015} note sidedressing can be combined with soil tests or crop N-deficiency sensing for real-time adjustment. Broader gaps include comprehensive volatilisation-loss studies, targeted research from data-poor regions, and provision of ancillary climate, yield, and soil data to support predictive modelling and policy \parencite{Chatterjee2020b}.

\subsubsection{Timing, form, and application method}

Timing and method of application remain recurring open questions. \textcite{Liang2018} propose combining timing with rate and note the value of models such as WHCNS for evaluating short- and long-term effects. \textcite{Malone2007b} highlight the need to quantify differences between split and single spring applications and to check possible over-prediction of winter nitrification and N loss for fall manure on soybean, while \textcite{HernandezRamirez2011} note it remains unknown whether reduced-rate fall manure could match sidedress or preplant applications environmentally, pointing to nitrification inhibitors, cover crops, more complex rotations, and multifunctional landscapes as additional avenues. \textcite{Elmi2005} stress quantifying all major N loss pathways simultaneously and obtaining better information on manure versus mineral systems. Product- and method-level questions are increasingly prominent: enhanced-efficiency fertiliser technology---spring-applied anhydrous ammonia with a nitrification inhibitor---should be tested in conjunction with enhanced DWM \parencite{Kaur2024c}; NH$_4^+$-N management via N stabilisers, and cover-crop effects on field- and watershed-scale NH$_4^+$-N dynamics, deserve exploration \parencite{Vincent2025}; and fertigation of silage corn requires study of whether drainage nitrate can be reduced without yield loss, given tradeoffs of crop injury, disease, and pathogen transport relative to slurry injection \parencite{Gamble2018}. Evening out manure application within high-livestock catchments, and reducing spring and autumn soil mineral N in cereal areas, are further priorities \parencite{Bechmann2014}. \textcite{Haghnazari2020} call for comparing the economic and environmental consequences of N use under global-warming projections.

\subsubsection{Crediting residual, mineralisable, and legacy nitrogen}

A recurring, more specific gap is the crediting of N supplied by soil organic matter, prior legumes, manure, and land-use legacies when setting rates. \textcite{Bakhsh2007,Bakhsh2005} call for assessing proper N credit from mineralisation of the soil organic-matter pool and soybean residue, and the storage of mineralisable N in the root zone, before applications. \textcite{Randall2001} extend this to improved soil-N testing methods to estimate mineralisable and carryover N, especially after dry years, legumes, or manure---echoed by a recommended spring soil-N test following dry years to credit residual nitrate \parencite{RANDALL1995} and by measuring mineral N to evaluate leaching potential before sowing \parencite{Conrad2009}. In systems transitioning from perennial cover, \textcite{Huggins2001} propose second-year N credits for mineralisation following grass conversion, and \textcite{ROBBINS1980} counsel care in setting rates and timing on land plowed out of alfalfa. For irrigated vulnerable soils, \textcite{Derby2009} recommend reducing N in early irrigation years, setting reasonable yield goals, crediting fall residual nitrate, and pursuing field-scale studies spanning soil, vadose-zone, and groundwater. The role of \emph{legacy} N is itself flagged as underexplored: a clearer definition of legacy nutrients is urgently needed to apportion losses \parencite{Williams2022}, and the coupled biogeochemical and hydrological processes regulating legacy-N formation and its impact on nitrate export in tile-drained agroecosystems require dedicated study \parencite{Hu2024}. Better quantification of fertiliser rates and timing, and reconsideration of fall application under warmer winters, would sharpen estimates of management influence on nitrate transport \parencite{Gentry2014}, and further quantification of soil N in growing and non-growing seasons is advised \parencite{Bosompemaa2021}. Emerging tooling---resin-membrane strips to characterise soil-N cycling \parencite{Masri2024}---and better predictions of long-term flow-weighted N concentrations for benchmark soils under typical rotations \parencite{Skaggs2012} round out this agenda.

\subsubsection{The temporal window of loss}

A distinctive frontier is the temporal concentration of losses, particularly in early spring, and the need for management targeted to that window. \textcite{Bakhsh2007} state that strategies must be developed to reduce early-spring nitrate leaching, and \textcite{Bakhsh2010} extend this to the March--June period, adding that the temporal distribution of growing-season rainfall needs further attention. Assessment of soil nitrate status, tile flow, and nitrate loss during April, May, and June warrants further study \parencite{Nance2007}, and a reliable winter-hardy cover crop ahead of corn, together with a shift of fall N to spring, may be the best way to decrease loss in intensively tile-drained watersheds \parencite{Gentry2024}. For outlet management, \textcite{Skaggs1995} recommend selectively managing drainage outlets to reduce peak nitrate losses, especially in years following drought-reduced yields, and both \textcite{Elmi2004,Elmi2002} note that keeping controlled drainage operational during early spring and late fall---when losses are most severe and drainage is not needed for production---could achieve further reductions.

\subsection{Phosphorus: metrics, mechanisms, transport, and prediction}

Phosphorus in subsurface drainage is repeatedly identified as under-researched relative to N. \textcite{Hertzberger2019} explicitly call for greater research effort on P transport and better understanding of nutrient-related water-quality issues, while \textcite{Yuan2022} note more research is needed on conservation-practice effectiveness in reducing dissolved-P loss in subsurface flow and on the causes of annual variability in reduction rates. \textcite{Pease2018} identify the pedologic factors influencing P transport and the effect of management on P loss as key needs across the Eastern Corn Belt, and \textcite{deOliveira2022} recommend refining P-loss frameworks through additional within-field studies, continued data collection on soil testing, site history, and agronomic management, and better understanding of the ``where'' and ``when'' of drainage P loss to sharpen conservation-practice timing and placement.

\subsubsection{Beyond agronomic soil-test P: saturation and sorption metrics}

A consistent theme is that agronomic soil-test P (STP) is insufficient for environmental risk. \textcite{Sharpley1995} argue that agronomic tests may not reliably assess eutrophication potential and that environmental tests estimating bioavailable P and sorption capacity should be applied to high-risk soils. \textcite{Hooda1999} propose that degree of P saturation (DPS) may better estimate loss potential than extractable P such as Olsen-P, and that a full accounting of all P output forms is needed; \textcite{Nguyen2002} call for establishing critical DPS values calibrated across sediment P tests and equilibrium P concentrations. \textcite{Osterholz2020} recommend assessing additional fields in the 100--400~mg~kg$^{-1}$ STP range and examining sorption saturation, macropore formation, and mineralogy, while P-adsorption capacity should be considered before organic soils are developed for cropping \parencite{MILLER1979}. \textcite{McDowell2001} propose that P isotope-exchange kinetics (IEK) has the potential to explain and eventually predict P loss to overland flow and subsurface drainage across a wide range of STP concentrations.

\subsubsection{Desorption, colloidal and particulate transport}

Multiple authors identify the sorption/desorption interface and particulate pathways as poorly resolved. \textcite{Mumbi2025} regard quantifying when and how varying water sources and fluxes interact with soil to desorb and adsorb P as critical to advancing prediction of subsurface DRP transport, and \textcite{Flores2021} note the need to understand mechanisms controlling particulate settling and P desorption. \textcite{Uusitalo2003} argue that directing runoff into the subsurface pathway and establishing field-margin buffers are only first steps, that mitigating bioavailable particulate P requires reducing particle mobilisation across the entire field surface, and that controlling diffuse DRP is a longer-term task than reducing erosion---with knowledge of the availability of eroded P to algae still limited \parencite{Ulén2007}. \textcite{Wilson2019} highlight the need to quantify the contribution of colloidal P to DRP measurements and to determine whether erosion-mitigating BMPs or nutrient management better control it, alongside clarifying stratification, preferential flow, and the effectiveness of subsurface P application. \textcite{Norgaard2013} recommend including both dissolved and colloid-associated leaching parameters in field-scale risk assessment. Sediment P release under prolonged flooding and alternating waterlogged--drying regimes \parencite{Nguyen2002}, P fractionation after leaching under polyacrylamide \parencite{Jiang2010}, and combining intact columns, plots, and catchments to examine subsurface loss of particulate matter and P in relation to macroporosity \parencite{Laubel1999} are all advanced as priorities.

\subsubsection{Management effects, events, ratios, and legacy P}

\textcite{Uusitalo2018} note that assessing the pace of P stratification requires more data, suggest simulation tools such as the Annual P Loss Estimator for evaluating mixing intervals, and leave open whether no-till adoption as a eutrophication-control measure is justified. \textcite{Ni2020} call for quantifying dominant mechanisms of agricultural conservation practices under complex scenarios and for studies of optimal soil-test-P levels under changing climate to address STP--precipitation interactions, while \textcite{Wang2018} ask whether pre- or immediate post-application tillage decreases DRP losses through tile drainage and whether results apply to manure injection. Manure-related risk---the immediate P loss after a single application---needs fuller understanding \parencite{Madison2014}. \textcite{Brendel2019} recommend designing BMPs that reduce P losses during frequent small events, understanding relationships between flow, precipitation, pathway, and P fraction, and using these to target implementation, and \textcite{Schilling2017b} call for refining organic-P/total-P ratios, quantifying wastewater-treatment-plant contributions, and identifying sources of variability such as manure and animal density. \textcite{Jarvie2017} ask whether changing climate contributes to increased soluble reactive P delivery, note it is unknown how much of a 40\% reduction target could be achieved by 4R practices alone, and warn that drawing down legacy P and disconnecting tile-flow P loads at times of maximum receiving-water sensitivity may be increasingly costly and intrusive. Cost-effective mitigation hierarchies are proposed, prioritising lower soil Olsen P and targeting alum to critical source areas \parencite{McDowell2015}, and P removal via regular grass harvesting under continuous-grass tile-drained fields \parencite{Zhang2015b}.

\subsubsection{Multi-species reporting, tillage, and databases}

Fragmented reporting hampers synthesis. \textcite{Christianson2016} call for more field-scale studies documenting drainage P losses across cropping, nutrient, soil, and design factors, including multiple P species (DRP, particulate, total), and for further developing the Drain Load database. \textcite{Macrae2019} seek understanding of hydroclimatic drivers of event responses and how tillage or P placement affects concentration relationships, and \textcite{Tanga2025} recommend monitoring both particulate and dissolved forms while selecting site-specific stoplog depths from slope, soil, and climate. \textcite{Zhang2017} add that impacts of modified no-till on DRP need investigation and that new soil-P testing and optimised application timing, rates, and methods are required under no-till. Modelling refinements proposed include adjustable manure-P mineralisation and decomposition parameters and further tillage-effect investigation \parencite{Pan2023}, continued P-loss model improvement and representation of crop stress prior to maturity \parencite{Wang2019}, and applicability testing of in-situ optical turbidimeters for particulate matter in drainage water \parencite{Grant1996}. \textcite{Daigh2015b} note that reactive-P concentrations and yields in bioenergy systems need further evaluation where P losses are high, and \textcite{Spiese2023} call for novel BMPs addressing both nitrate and DRP simultaneously.

\subsubsection{Phosphorus in organic (Histosol/peat) soils}

Phosphorus in cultivated organic soils is singled out as poorly understood. \textcite{Grenon2022} call for mechanistic studies of the pathways, hot spots, and hot moments of P transfer from agricultural organic soils, including residual-fraction dynamics, long-term soil--water study, and sorption dynamics for accurate balances, and note that more frequent post-fertilisation sampling and isotope tracers would help. \textcite{Grenon2021} propose a concrete agenda: assessing P geochemistry in Histosols using isotope tracing ($^{32}$P/$^{33}$P and oxygen isotopes in phosphates), XANES, and $^{31}$P~NMR alongside sequential fractionation; assessing microbial turnover; long-term DWM studies to reduce total-P loads; a new model accounting for geo-mechanical instability; and P-transport indices. Nutrient leaching from abandoned peat fields is likewise poorly studied, calling for research into ditch blockage, energy-willow, or forestation, and into the true nature of H$_2$SO$_4$-P via P K-edge XANES \parencite{Höyhtyä2025}.

\subsection{Cropping systems: cover crops, perennials, rotations, and diversification}

\subsubsection{Cover crops: agronomic, environmental, and modelling frontiers}

Cover crops---especially winter rye---attract the largest concentration of open questions. \textcite{Kladivko2014} call directly for further research, development, and technical assistance to overcome practical obstacles to adoption, for additional field experiments on multiple soil types across the Midwest, and for using new data to calibrate and validate RZWQM. \textcite{Birru2023} and \textcite{Zhao2016} stress that existing evidence is drawn from too few, geographically clustered sites and call for extending scope across more species, soils, climates, and management. On the agronomic side, priorities include tools to promote rye growth in colder regions and long-term no-till study \parencite{Waring2020}, strategies to establish rye under temperature, precipitation, and growing-degree-day extremes \parencite{Bawa2023}, evaluation of more cultivars and improved establishment to reduce year-to-year variability and yield impacts \parencite{Kaspar2007}, cultural and plant-breeding research to improve compatibility and cold hardiness \parencite{Dinnes2002,Liu2019}, and investigation of whether observed yield drag reflects poor planter performance through residue \parencite{Dougherty2020}. Kura clover as living mulch with corn requires better agronomic management to avoid yield loss \parencite{Qi2011b,Qi2011}.

On environmental mechanisms, authors call for research on the release of scavenged N in spring and its synchronisation with corn and soybean growth stages \parencite{Lacey2015}, producer-adoptable methods to determine cover-crop N content and adjustable seeding rates \parencite{Roth2018}, the combined effects of cover crops and manure rate on subsurface DRP \parencite{Askar2023}, and optimisation of the trade-offs between cover-crop services and disservices for the water balance \parencite{Meyer2019}. \textcite{Liu2019} more broadly call for coordinated common experiments across geophysical conditions to quantify winter-cover P contributions, identify reliable analytes, and predict future-climate influences. Longer-term evidence remains scarce: additional long-term studies of winter cover-crop effectiveness \parencite{Kaspar2012}, longer study of rye influence on soil-health parameters \parencite{Bawa2023}, and understanding of the long-term consequences and biotic--abiotic mechanisms by which rye affects yield and environmental performance \parencite{MartinezFeria2016} are all sought. Optimising cereal-rye management \parencite{deOliveira2022}, growing cover crops longer in spring to prevent N$_2$O increases \parencite{PrezaFontes2023}, and identifying practices that target individual nutrient pathways because over-winter effects differ for N versus P \parencite{McNeill2026} are further priorities, while \textcite{Krueger2013} recommend conservation practices beyond rye because subsurface nitrate remained high and rye had no effect on DRP or drainage volume.

Cover-crop \emph{modelling} is a distinct frontier. Needed advances include validation of the rye growth model and better plant-N-uptake algorithms \parencite{Feyereisen2006}, thorough multi-year testing to fix winter cover-crop parameters and more realistic N-uptake routines \parencite{Malone2007c}, improved simulation of rye germination and establishment \parencite{Malone2014b}, a dedicated winter-rye crop module and careful calibration of soil-organic-matter and drained-upper-limit parameters \parencite{Chatterjee2025}, correction of APSIM weaknesses in cover-crop effects on following corn, frozen-soil temperatures, and soybean drainage \parencite{Dietzel2016}, a chemical-termination routine \parencite{Gupta2022}, and improved drainage and mineralisation/immobilisation modules with attention to extreme events \parencite{Gupta2023b}. Additional species such as radish and legumes are proposed for hydrology and water-quality simulation \parencite{Singh2023}, and lysimeter-paired field validation of rye effects on soil-water dynamics and nitrate leaching is underway \parencite{Qi2010b}.

\subsubsection{Perennials, bioenergy, and rotational diversification}

Perennial and land-use-change options feature prominently. Poorly drained depressions are proposed as candidates for perennial bioenergy crops such as \textit{Miscanthus $\times$ giganteus} or wetland restoration \parencite{Huang2023}, and keeping soil covered year-round with a growing crop such as perennial ley is judged the most effective way to mitigate N losses, while the P focus should be on decreasing surface runoff \parencite{Norberg2022}. Additional data on alfalfa's effect on subsurface discharge during large events across diverse geographies are sought \parencite{Arrueta2025}, and a clear causal link between perennial-crop implementation and nutrient loads across soils is called for, alongside policies rewarding environmental services \parencite{Arrueta2022}. Robust observed water-quality datasets for perennialised systems \parencite{Wilson2023}, large-scale assessment of bioenergy-crop scenarios with cost--benefit analysis to alleviate Gulf of Mexico hypoxia \parencite{Guo2018}, and research on the value of and payments for externalities such as P, sediment, greenhouse-gas, and biodiversity benefits of switchgrass \parencite{Woodbury2018} extend this agenda. Rotation and diversification questions recur: constraining crop-rotation impacts on yield-scaled N loss and accounting for losses on a rotation rather than annual basis \parencite{Zhao2016}, the paucity of research on desirable rotation crops in two-crop Midwestern systems \parencite{Pasley2021}, alternative perennial-containing systems and partial conversion of cropland to pasture or wetland to attain nitrate TMDLs \parencite{Randall2001,Gowda2007,Nangia2008}, and explicit evaluation of controlled drainage, rotation, cover crops, and N management together \parencite{Ma2007b}. Novel options such as intercropping triticale with red clover and developing winter triticale \parencite{Nance2007}, camelina--soybean relay- and double-cropping \parencite{Emmett2022}, fertilised/harvested rye for revenue and net energy \parencite{Malone2018,Malone2023}, and a diverse cropping system documented against the conventional two-year rotation \parencite{Gentry2025} are all advanced for study. \textcite{Dinnes2002} call broadly for perennial cash-cropping systems that maintain returns while reducing leaching, and \textcite{Jaynes2001} conclude that dramatic management change on artificially drained soils will be required before corn production can be considered sustainable.

\subsection{Drainage water management, water-table control, and system design}

Controlled drainage (CD), shallow drainage, subirrigation, and other water-table management approaches are seen as promising but requiring optimisation, verification across climates, and clearer mechanistic partitioning. \textcite{Jaynes2012} are unaware of research optimising DWM operation for the soils, climate, and crops of central Iowa, and call for identifying locations where more field area falls under control-structure influence and for cost-sharing incentives. \textcite{Fang2012} stress that careful management of gate timing and depth is essential to optimise reduced N loss, and \textcite{Wesström2001} argue that water balances for conventional and controlled plots must be established before differing outflow patterns can be separated. Whether managed drainage can consistently produce greater yield than free drainage---perhaps concentrated in higher-yield-potential years on claypan soils---remains unresolved \parencite{Nash2015,Nelson2011}, and continued study in normal-precipitation years may clarify whether CD offers water-conservation advantages and whether shallow drainage reduces nitrate \parencite{Smith2011b}. CD adoption may become more relevant under wetter, warmer climates, requiring practical control-structure management during wet springs \parencite{Madramootoo2024}, and the collective impacts of automated DWM on the coupled timing of flows and composition of exported water need study before its full BMP efficacy can be assessed \parencite{Miller2022}. Optimum dormant-season management depth needs more experience \parencite{Adeuya2012}, better on-site monitoring, long calibration periods, and anti-seep collars are recommended \parencite{Gunn2015}, and economic and agronomic questions---yield effects via SEW$_{30}$, planting-date impacts, and the economic risk of reducing nitrate loss with DWM---remain open \parencite{Schott2017b}.

Optimising drainage design for the joint goals of yield and water quality is a persistent problem. \textcite{Kladivko2021} argue that more field studies coupled with modelling are urgently needed to develop site-specific recommendations and optimise design for both yield and water quality, and \textcite{Zhao2016} suggest research into alternative drain spacing balancing production against decreased losses. Field experiments characterising how shallow and controlled drainage affect the complete water balance, including soil-moisture-based flexible control \parencite{Singh2006b,Singh2007b}, low-cost retrofittable water-table-control designs \parencite{Dinnes2002}, and subirrigation extended to diverse regions with in-depth economic assessment \parencite{Gunn2019,Baker2004} are all sought. Cold-climate and peatland settings raise specific needs: optimising winter subsurface drainage and water-table management as temperatures warm \parencite{Li2025}, evaluating subsurface irrigation to maintain shallow water tables and mapping cultivated peatlands \parencite{Läpikivi2025}, and cautions that severe frost may irreversibly affect ploughed-soil structure \parencite{STEINHARDT1974}. Drainage \emph{materials and installation} also feature: long-term investigation of knitted-sock envelope clogging \parencite{Ghane2022}, straw and crop-residue drainage materials and integrated excavation-and-burial machinery \parencite{Qi2025b,Lu2018}, DRAINMOD-based evaluation of design impacts on production, salinity, and waterlogging \parencite{Ellafi2021}, countermeasures such as combining drainage with raised-bed planting \parencite{Takeshima2023}, and perforation-characteristic and standardisation studies for drain materials \parencite{Zhang2025b,ASGHAR1995}. Nutrient-trading programmes are identified as needed to increase shallow-drain adoption \parencite{Ghane2021b}, and in paddy and rice systems new water-management options may reshape agronomy and require new infrastructure and precision integration \parencite{DarziNaftchali2014,Jung2024,Hashemi2020}.

\subsection{Edge-of-field and in-field treatment technologies}

Engineered nitrate- and P-removal structures---bioreactors, saturated riparian buffers (SRBs), constructed wetlands, and filter-based drainage---constitute a frontier where design optimisation and long-term field performance remain open. \textcite{McEachran2020} state that a key challenge is to predict nitrate removal so that more effective SRBs can be designed, outlining methodological advances (unsteady governing equations, average linear velocity, numerical nitrate transport, and optimal-width approximation). \textcite{Jaynes2014} propose that replicating SRB results across landscapes could prevent substantial nitrate from entering midwestern streams and that biomass harvesting might maintain long-term viability, while \textcite{Abdalaal2023} recommend more sophisticated removal and hydrology components, coupling design with economic or objective-function targets, and determining maximum allowable buffer length before optimising width. Confirming that SRB N removal increases as vegetation matures, and quantifying gains from individual buffer components with monitoring wells and backflow valves, are further needs \parencite{Jaynes2019,Singh2024b}. For bioreactors and combined installations, \textcite{Husk2017} call for study of CD with and without a denitrification bioreactor to quantify each component, note that treating greater volumes at lower efficiency could improve mass removal, and warn that clogging potential must be evaluated. Refining denitrification-bioreactor design---reactor size, packing materials, equilibrium thresholds, elimination of preferential flow, and pH maintenance---is emphasised \parencite{Aide2016}, as are vegetation and organic matrices in reactive riparian zones \parencite{Vallée2014}, wetland restoration or denitrifying ponds to treat drainage before it enters ditches \parencite{Randall2001}, and a broad agenda for constructed treatment systems spanning driving factors, flow--treatment relationships, design-parameter effects, long-term field tests, and winter-efficiency enhancement \parencite{Mendes2021}. For filter-based drainage, nitrification and denitrification in soil and filter cannot be neglected over long operation, and long-term experiments with different filter materials are needed \parencite{Tao2022}.

\subsection{Bundling, sequencing, spatial targeting, and critical source areas}

A pervasive conclusion is that no single BMP suffices and that combinations must be studied, sequenced, and optimised. \textcite{Sunohara2016} state plainly that no one BMP is a silver bullet, and \textcite{Lenhart2016} argue that multiple BMPs in series are needed, that in-field/edge-of-field combinations show great promise but have been little studied, and that wetlands and water-retention BMPs must be developed for sensitive downstream waters. Attaining water-quality goals will require a combination of practices implemented at a high level in a targeted manner \parencite{Christianson2014}. Combining conservation tillage with cover crops, pairing in-field with edge-of-field conservation, and incorporating legacy effects \parencite{Makhtoumi2025}, examining synergistic effects and formalising optimisation studies \parencite{Kreig2019}, and better understanding of practice interaction and the N-removal capacity of various practices with improved mapping of tile-drained land \parencite{McLellan2015} are all advanced. \textcite{Baulch2019} lay out a broad cold-region programme---comparing surface, tile, and undrained fields; studying CD, channel design, and maintenance through wet--dry cycles; assessing tillage and perennial-forage conversion; quantifying retention in reservoirs, wetlands, and buffers; and developing snowmelt-suited tile-drainage mitigation. Wider application of bioreactors, P reactors, CD, and low-impact development, potentially alongside internal-loading management \parencite{Husk2024,Husk2017}, robust cost-effective BMPs to support cover crops and mitigate warming \parencite{Gupta2023}, and intercrops and hydrology-altering management for P-transport reduction \parencite{HernandezRamirez2011,GAYNOR1995b} extend the bundling agenda.

Closely related is targeting practices to the parts of the landscape that matter most. \textcite{Melland2008} recommend identifying hydrologically active areas as mitigation priorities and avoiding high stocking and inputs in waterlogged runoff-generating zones. Whether elevated loads originate from numerous low-intensity or a few key hot spots \parencite{Irvine2019}, whether targeted or regionally saturated cover-crop implementation is required for consistent nitrate and soluble reactive P reductions \parencite{Speir2022}, and the delineation of zones transporting higher nitrate leaching that require site-specific management \parencite{Bakhsh2005b} are all open questions. Targeting critical source areas and hydrologically connected hotspots is repeatedly stressed \parencite{Baulch2019,McDowell2015,Brendel2019}, prioritisation is expected to improve return on conservation investment \parencite{Makhtoumi2025}, and automating spatial-targeting principles into an easily applicable GIS tool with whole-basin surveys and cost--benefit analysis is prioritised \parencite{Kvítek2023}. Combining GIS with hydrological modelling and saturation risk to assess DRP and nitrate loss, and finding the nitrate threshold across P saturation, texture, and temperature, are further scaling priorities \parencite{Smith2023}, alongside better understanding of the coupled effect of controlling factors and drainage density on stream solute dynamics \parencite{Kennedy2012} and identification of critical precipitation periods rather than annual values \parencite{Salo2006}.

\subsection{Process-based modelling: outstanding algorithmic and evaluation gaps}

Because field trials cannot cover all combinations of practice, soil, and climate, process-based models are expected to fill the gap, yet the literature documents extensive, still-unresolved modelling needs. \textcite{Du2017} anticipate models will be relied upon extensively as concern over nitrate export increases, and \textcite{SKAGGS1994} caution that much remains to be learned about the mechanisms governing pollutant losses, that process understanding must improve before quantification is possible, and that better datasets are needed to test models across wide ranges of soil, crop, and climate. \textcite{Smith2008} argue that a validated process-based rather than empirical model should be applied to capture complex variable interactions.

\subsubsection{Soil carbon--nitrogen cycling, plant uptake, and crop growth}

Many authors identify soil N-process representation as the weakest link. Priorities include improving nitrification, denitrification, fermentation, and mineralisation within an improved hydrology framework \parencite{Smith2020}, better representing crop N uptake, soil N cycling, and C--N pool transformations to reduce errors in flow-weighted nitrate predictions \parencite{Fang2012}, and improving soil N processes, soybean N fixation, and maize-yield simulation in SWAT \parencite{Hu2007}. RZWQM improvement agendas recur throughout: accounting for subsurface lateral flow below tiles, simulating rather than supplying soybean N fixation, improving plant N uptake, and reducing winter nitrification so more NH$_4$-N remains at planting \parencite{Ma2007}; better simulation of management effects on yield and mineralisation and extension to additional tile-drained sites \parencite{Ma2007b}; improved crop responses to N-application timing and rigorous evaluation of each C/N process before microbial-growth roles can be understood \parencite{Ma2008}; and a wide-ranging programme spanning variable cracking, P dynamics for manure, mobile organic-matter movement, new-crop parameterisation, standardised calibration, and GIS/economic linkage \parencite{Ma2001}. Modifying SWAT nutrient-cycling algorithms for immobilisation under corn-stover removal \parencite{Malone2019}, revising winter processes and organic-C mineralisation in the PNM model \parencite{Marjerison2016}, a more robust approach to grass biomass and N uptake in drained grassland \parencite{Bechtold2007}, refining N-cycling for N$_2$ fixation and plant uptake to correct soybean over-prediction \parencite{Bakhsh2004}, and a more physically based tile-nitrate model using ``soft data'' and a passive humus pool \parencite{Qi2025} complete this cluster. Systematic data collection on water and solute fluxes, greenhouse-gas emissions, and root and biomass development is repeatedly identified as the foundation for model validation and refinement \parencite{Ma2025}.

\subsubsection{Winter, frozen-soil, and snow processes}

Cold-season representation is singled out as inadequate. Better algorithms for soil freezing/thawing and spring-thaw drainage, and more detailed mechanistic plant-N-uptake and snow--freeze--thaw representation, are needed \parencite{Feyereisen2006b}. DRAINMOD-N needs improvement for freezing and thawing seasons and additional inputs such as tillage and intercropping \parencite{Yang2007}, and winter processes in seasonally frozen regions warrant investigation \parencite{Saadat2020,Jiang2020}. An impedance factor for frozen-soil hydraulic conductivity, ice-content measurements, and a calibratable snow--rain temperature threshold are proposed for the RZ-SHAW model \parencite{Li2024b}, and continued development and testing of RZWQM for winter conditions---including snow algorithms and improved water-retention parameterisation---is recommended \parencite{Ahmed2007b,Ahmed2007,Yang2021}. Swelling and shrinkage during infiltration into frozen soil \parencite{Roy2020} and improved winter-crop parameterisation with macropore infiltration evaluation \parencite{Motarjemi2021} round out this agenda.

\subsubsection{Macropores, preferential flow, and drain configuration}

\textcite{Smith2020} recommend improving runoff simulation under surface crusting, clay cracking, preferential flow, snow dynamics, and freeze--thaw, and \textcite{Stamm2002} argue that more attention should be given to actual drain configuration to complete the picture of solute transport in drained soils. SIDRA-RU upgrades---a new nitrate-transport model, sampling-interval sensitivity analysis, crop-rotation coupling, and time-variant parameter estimation \parencite{Chelil2022b,Jeantet2021}---and runoff models that include temporal variability in hydrological-pathway activation for cracking clay soils \parencite{Kokulan2019} are proposed. \textcite{Boivin2006} call for focus on hydraulic-conductivity and shrink-swell parameters and their seasonal time-dependence, direct in-field measurement methods, and delineation of seasonal preferential-flow processes.

\subsubsection{Drain-design decision support, coupling, and validation}

\textcite{Moriasi2013} propose using the Revised SWAT model to determine optimum drain depth and spacing but stress that validation of the Hooghoudt and Kirkham equations is needed before use as a decision-support tool. Validating nitrate-load methodologies against DRAINMOD-NII or RZWQM and in additional watersheds \parencite{Ale2012}, multi-year calibration for free-drainage treatments \parencite{Ng2000}, additional testing across soils, crops, and locations before routine application \parencite{Skaggs1995}, and using process-based models to feed databases for economic and risk analysis \parencite{Malone2007} are all advanced. Broader model-development needs include intense field measurement for validation and long-term climate-change simulation \parencite{Negm2014}, improved prediction of high-load peak events \parencite{Grewal2025}, DRAINMOD--DSSAT coupling to capture factors influencing maize relative yield under subirrigation \parencite{Gunn2018}, greater sensitivity to low flows and macropore-flow algorithms \parencite{Morrison2014}, incorporation of CO$_2$, disease, cultivar, and planting-date effects \parencite{Ndulue2024}, additional crop-genetic-parameter calibration and elevated-CO$_2$ accounting for winter crops \parencite{Li2025}, and improved subsurface-drainage simulation within soybean in APSIM \parencite{Dietzel2016}.

\subsubsection{Groundwater interactions and integrated transport}

Coupling drainage models to groundwater is a distinct need. \textcite{Addab2022} recommend SWAT-MODFLOW-RT3D for physically based flow and reactive transport once a salinity module is incorporated, and \textcite{Hoang2017} argue that improving SWAT's groundwater module to simulate water-table fluctuation and groundwater--surface-water exchange is necessary to represent nitrate retention in riparian zones realistically. Optimal regulation requires considering both spatial variation in N leaching and variation in N reduction across groundwater and surface-water systems \parencite{Hojberg2017}. Integrated mathematical models for surface-water--groundwater nutrient transport, with machine-learning application and climate-change evaluation \parencite{Elsayed2023}, defensible site-specific water balances to estimate the split between artificial drainage and groundwater flows \parencite{Barkle2021}, and improved NH$_4$-N transport and transformation modelling \parencite{Yang2008} are further priorities. Specialised reactive-transport extensions are also sought---for lime precipitation/dissolution and trace-element reactivity in salinity- and trace-element-affected systems \parencite{KARAJEH1994c}, and clay-mineralogy incorporation with field validation \parencite{Dadfar2010}.

\subsection{Greenhouse gases, emerging contaminants, and multi-objective tradeoffs}

An expanding frontier integrates greenhouse-gas (GHG) and contaminant considerations into drainage-mitigation assessment, especially the coupling between nitrate leaching and indirect nitrous oxide (N$_2$O). \textcite{Sawamoto2003} call for studies of the nitrate--N$_2$O relationship in groundwater and of indirect N$_2$O from subsurface drainage, and \textcite{Minamikawa2015} argue that new calculation methods for indirect N$_2$O from leaching and runoff are needed, along with evaluation of the tradeoff between indirect N$_2$O and N removal by heterotrophic denitrification across groundwater, drainage, river, and estuary \parencite{Minamikawa2013,Minamikawa2010}. Continued investigation of nitrate--N$_2$O linkages when N application and precipitation are temporally disconnected \parencite{OBrien2022}, the magnitude and fate of N$_2$O losses \parencite{Burton2024}, whether dissolved N$_2$O losses exceed surface emissions \parencite{Smith2019}, and long-term cumulative dissolved-GHG effects of management \parencite{Oliveira2025} are all open. \textcite{Webb2021} outline an artificial-water emissions-accounting agenda---seasonal emission-factor impacts, process-based modelling, gas-transfer-velocity models, the DOC/NO$_3$ ratio, and a global database---and \textcite{Fontes2019} recommend year-round monitoring of both N$_2$O and nitrate leaching for better total-N-loss estimates. Net-GHG evaluation in longer experiments \parencite{PrezaFontes2023}, the fate of waterborne methane and inclusion of GHGs alongside P balances when selecting measures \parencite{Carstensen2021}, and comparative life-cycle assessment based on complete N balances \parencite{HernandezRamirez2011} extend this frontier. In cold and poorly drained soils, biogeochemical measurement and modelling of methane production and oxidation is likewise needed \parencite{Kumar2014b}.

Emerging contaminants and biological interactions are also flagged. Better constraint of the mechanism and lifetime of glyphosate in soil and water \parencite{Spiese2023}, investigation of planter modifications to reduce aquatic exposure to neonicotinoid and other seed-applied pesticides \parencite{Schaafsma2019}, understanding of how watershed microbial communities respond to and control nutrient fluxes \parencite{Knorr2023}, and study of water-quality changes during different rainfall-runoff events to identify compound sources \parencite{Fraindová2022} are all advanced. Particulate silicon transport and its coupling to warming, phytolith mineralisation, and diatom dynamics \parencite{Sethna2022}, nutrient stoichiometry and the fate of organic matter in aquatic ecosystems \parencite{Manninen2023,Manninen2018}, and the release of accumulated compounds and antibiotic-resistant genes in sludge-amended soils \parencite{Ghirardini2019} broaden the constituent scope. Overarching tradeoffs remain unresolved: the tradeoff of increased nitrate losses under some practices \parencite{Derby2024}, the balance between crop-yield gains in tiled areas and downstream water-quality losses \parencite{Addab2022}, and unresolved tradeoffs among N-loss pathways when switching to fertigation \parencite{Gamble2018}. Emission- and load-optimisation is expected to hinge on optimal N rate and timing that minimise yield--environment tradeoffs \parencite{Emmett2022}.

\subsection{Monitoring, tracers, isotopes, and source apportionment}

Improved measurement and tracing underpin many proposed advances. \textcite{Williams2022} call for higher-frequency (sub-daily to daily) measurements, further work on groundwater dynamics in DRP transport, and more comprehensive source-apportionment methods, and \textcite{Hu2024} recommend quantifying $\delta^{18}$O of nitrification-derived nitrate across environments and coupling water and nitrate oxygen isotopes to quantify evaporative enrichment. Monitoring spatial heterogeneity and temporal variability of nitrate concentrations and isotopic compositions in topsoil, drains, and ditches, using multiple tracers, is deemed essential to characterise soil's contribution to river nitrate \parencite{Briand2017}, and boron-isotope analyses around feedlots and fields are advanced to strengthen contaminant tracing \parencite{Komor1997}. Combining isotopic and pathway-partitioning approaches---explicit reporting of surface-inlet presence and partitioning of water and nutrient loads by pathway via before-after control-impact designs, hydrograph separation, or tracers \parencite{Flores2021}, evaluating overland-flow versus tile-flow contributions to edge-of-field losses \parencite{Kokulan2019}, and stable water isotopes to verify groundwater flow paths and identify plant water sources \parencite{Islam2022,YliHalla2022}---is repeatedly urged. The role of groundwater outflow in nutrient loading is flagged as under-quantified: modelling and measurement of its contribution \parencite{Turunen2013}, its inclusion in future N-loss studies with attention to non-mineral fractions \parencite{Jokinen2024}, and accounting for the redox status of underlying groundwater when estimating total-N export \parencite{Barkle2021} are all needed. Finer temporal analyses---interactions between drain flow and concentration variation \parencite{Lagzdins2015}, whether near-term flow-weighted nitrate changes predict long-term load and yield changes \parencite{Danalatos2022}, and detailed storm-hydrograph analysis to test event-scale hypotheses \parencite{Kladivko2004}---complete this methodological frontier.

\subsection{Soil health, biology, and drainage-induced soil evolution}

Slower-acting soil and biological processes are repeatedly flagged as understudied. The soil-biology response to changing drainage hydrology requires longer-term evaluation of biological indicators under standardised sampling \parencite{Sherbine2023}, study of the interaction mechanisms between subsurface drainage and soil physicochemical properties and microbial activity \parencite{Yang2025}, and continued research on microbial community structure and function to explain tillage and drainage impacts \parencite{Kaur2024b,Kumar2014}. Scalable nematode measurement integrated with a soil-health index \parencite{Li2023b}, valid and repeatable soil-quality indicators within a systems framework \parencite{Nakajima2015}, soil-enzyme activities as management diagnostics \parencite{Singh2007}, proof that no-till improves soil-health functions enough to reduce nitrate leaching sustainably \parencite{Masri2024}, and dynamic compaction indicators with long-term forage or agroforestry restoration trials \parencite{Caron2025} are all advanced. Specific biological questions include the link between \textit{Fusarium} abundance and soybean root rot under long-term drainage \parencite{Han2017}, disruption of crack-to-burrow linkages through conservation tillage \parencite{Shipitalo2004}, precise evapotranspiration quantification to explain drain-flow differences \parencite{Daigh2014}, drought tolerance and phenology of rice cultivars guiding drainage-system selection \parencite{DarziNaftchali2017b}, and forage-sorghum nitrate testing before grazing \parencite{Nash2017}.

A distinctive line of inquiry treats subsurface drainage as a driver of long-term, potentially chaotic soil evolution. \textcite{Montagne2009} argue that quantifying human-induced soil evolution as a function of time should be a research priority, including drainage's impact on clay-mineral dissolution, and that soil evolution in response to climate change must be explicitly studied and incorporated in global models \parencite{Montagne2010}; significant material losses over a few decades make human-induced perturbation a powerful tool for studying soil-evolution timescales \parencite{Montagne2013}. Whether drainage-induced evolution continues or stabilises over decades, with measurements along drains at different slope positions and network ages, remains open \parencite{Frison2009}, and a long classical experiment continues to test whether soil structure is eventually affected \parencite{HOOGHOUDT1952}. Empirical prediction of earthworm activity from tillage, residue cover, and particle size---likely stronger for no-till than conventional systems---is proposed \parencite{Ouellet2008}, and maintaining drainage operation and cultivation is framed as a management imperative for long-term soil health \parencite{Singh2007}.

\subsection{Salinity, acid sulfate, reclamation, and irrigation-return management}

In arid, saline, and irrigated settings, distinct research and management needs emerge. \textcite{Sharma2004} suggest sequential reuse across cereals, grasses, forest plantations, and halophytes and innovative evaporation-pond designs, while gypsum, elemental sulphur, and lime amendments are recommended for long-term management of problem soils \parencite{MANN1982,He2015}. Tolerant rootstocks, halophyte germplasm, and transgenic crops \parencite{Cuevas2019}, land-treatment/pond combinations with restricted hydraulic loading \parencite{Christen2010}, pre-treatment and preferential-flow control before wastewater application \parencite{Duan2015}, and biochar selected for low contaminant content with urgent long-term impact assessment \parencite{Tian2023b} are all advanced, alongside optimum rates of organic soil conditioners to minimise adverse water-quality impacts \parencite{Zhang2015}. Modelling and management priorities include long-term geohydrological and crop data to validate simulations across irrigation/drainage systems \parencite{Yao2017}, systematic evaluation of alternative field-scale irrigation and drainage strategies under ambiguity with a statistical likelihood of achieving goals \parencite{Alzraiee2013}, land-use planning steering irrigation toward low-salinity aquifers \parencite{Bethune2008}, salt-tolerant crops in reclamation rotations \parencite{Qadir2001}, and quantification of rainfall and irrigation-efficiency gains relative to salt-removal capacity \parencite{Castanheira2010}. Water-productivity and technology questions recur: HYDRUS--EPIC evaluation of saline-water-irrigation sustainability \parencite{Feng2019}, water-table contributions to storage and evapotranspiration \parencite{Jia2017}, scalable data infrastructure, life-cycle cost assessment, and low-temperature crystallisation toward near-zero discharge \parencite{Qin2025}, sensor- and expert-informed scheduling \parencite{Abdelkrim2021}, complete economic evaluation of irrigation-technology shifts \parencite{DINAR1989}, and continued monitoring of yield and water-quality change to forecast groundwater suitability \parencite{RADSTAKE1988,Karegoudar2022,Qian2025,ElHassanin2022,Ayars2006}. Sediment- and return-flow reduction from furrow-irrigated fields, improved pond removal, and nitrogen-leaching-limiting irrigation and nutrient management are prioritised \parencite{Bjorneberg2020}.

Drained acid sulfate (AS) and boreal soils raise specialised questions: short-term hydro-meteorological effects on sulfide oxidation, ripening effects on hydraulic properties, and secondary-mineral formation should be incorporated into model applications \parencite{Salo2023}; the role of the N cycle in sulfide oxidation is unknown in boreal cultivated AS soils \parencite{Virtanen2017}; the peat-cover thickness required to hinder sulfidic-material oxidation needs detailed study \parencite{YliHalla2022}; and more detailed AS-soil maps, understanding of anthropogenic versus organic acidity, and cessation of drainage of potential AS soils are urged \parencite{Åström2005}. For reclaimed saline, waterlogged, and Vertisol systems, priorities include how subsurface drainage integrated with crop and residue management affects soil quality, carbon sequestration, nutrient budgets, and GHG fluxes \parencite{Mukhopadhyay2023,Mukhopadhyay2023b}, long-term effects of controlled drainage on salinity \parencite{ElGhannam2023}, salt-crust formation and dissolved-species transport \parencite{Colombani2016}, leaching effects on additional elements and organic modifiers \parencite{SoltaniGerdefaramarzi2020}, monitoring of elemental toxicities and waterlogging-stage-specific drainage scheduling \parencite{Qian2021}, long-term root-growth resistance and drainable-porosity determination \parencite{Bahçeci2018,Bahceci2008}, additive-effectiveness and land-replacement characterisation \parencite{Isa2023}, and further multi-location, multi-condition testing over longer periods \parencite{Sadhukhan2019}. Retired land over shallow water tables surrounded by irrigated fields raises unresolved ultimate-use questions \parencite{Purkey2001}.

\subsection{Remote sensing, precision tools, decision support, and data infrastructure}

Emerging sensing and data technologies are widely seen as enabling the next phase of research. \textcite{Khanal2020} outline a broad agenda: optimal conditions for detecting tile-drainage patterns and automatic extraction of drain lines; finer-resolution soil moisture from multi-source downscaling; remote sensing coupled with machine learning for soil properties and compaction; within-field nitrogen-stress mapping for variable-rate N; and semi-supervised and generative approaches to weed identification and site-specific residue management. \textcite{Cassman2025} propose a spatial framework combining climate and soil type with geo-referenced farmer-reported data, tailoring a Technology Extrapolation Domain framework and using remote sensing and on-farm databases to estimate field-specific benefits. \textcite{Reid2018} call for validating aggregate edge-of-field losses for Canadian conditions, quantifying edge-of-field-to-surface-water delivery ratios, and intensity-based P-loss indicators, and \textcite{Julien2023} envision a soil-wetness-index approach within a decision-support tool, noting drainage-season and threshold assumptions need testing across soils. Integrated water-and-nutrient management with advancing precision agriculture \parencite{Jung2024}, remote-sensing testing for environmental monitoring \parencite{Fontes2019}, and downscaled climate information with better forecasts and understanding of how farmers incorporate excess-precipitation experience into decisions \parencite{Morton2015} extend the tooling frontier.

Numerous studies frame the frontier in terms of improved indices, tools, and shared data. Development of a phosphorus index for German conditions incorporating subsurface drainage, peat groundwater transport, and stream-network connectivity, possibly combined with an N index \parencite{Buczko2007}, and improvement of nutrient-index schemes to include wetland and riparian-buffer removal capacity and catchment soil wetness \parencite{Pärn2012} are proposed. Regression-based tools for nutrient-credit trading via DWM can be extended to other regions with user-friendly interfaces \parencite{Negm2016}, and modifying the Soil Vulnerability Index to differentiate surface from subsurface artificial drainage and incorporate irrigation and rainfall intensity is advanced \parencite{Baffaut2020}. Stress s-curve refinement for aeration effects on yield \parencite{Wang2016}, regionally adjustable yield-loss coefficients \parencite{ROBINSON1992}, calibration and validation across multiple sub-watersheds \parencite{Singh2023}, coupling models with tools such as INVEST to assess ecosystem-service tradeoffs \parencite{McLellan2015}, and focusing conservation assessment on managing potholes that drain to ditches \parencite{Smith2008} round out the modelling-and-data infrastructure agenda. Underlying all of these is the data imperative: better, longer, and more spatially representative datasets---spanning water and solute fluxes, GHG emissions, root and biomass development, and management history---are the foundational requirement for validating models, resolving tradeoffs, and delivering site-specific, climate-resilient guidance \parencite{Ma2025,SKAGGS1994,Deng2011}.

\subsection{Climate change, extremes, and system resilience}

Climate adaptation frames many priorities. \textcite{Saha2024} stress continuing to identify practices and combinations that increase the resilience of agricultural systems to climate extremes across additional soils and longer periods, and \textcite{Malone2020} lay out a modelling agenda addressing additional periods, representation concentration pathways, management options, cultivars, cover-crop types, and soils. Within-county variation in fertiliser rate, crop-specific manure applications, and multiple global-climate-model forcings from CMIP5 or CMIP6, with attention to perennial-grass co-benefits, are advanced \parencite{Ferin2023}, and cropping systems designed to function under both drought and flood---with engineering solutions such as capturing excess floodwater---are prioritised \parencite{Dietzel2016}. Costs and benefits of adaptation to changing precipitation, irrigation-investment payback, and carbon-farming knowledge-sharing \parencite{PeltonenSainio2021}, study of extreme-event effects on cover-crop impacts for field-scale adaptation \parencite{Gupta2023b}, and comparison of N-fertiliser consequences under warming with best field-management identification \parencite{Haghnazari2020} extend the climate frontier. Testing and refinement across additional rainfall environments, soils, and rotations---to confirm interactions between subsurface drainage and planting pattern \parencite{Takeshima2023}, validate pipe-burial-depth effects on water productivity \parencite{Liu2025}, and characterise responses across soil and rainfall levels \parencite{Grewal2025,Negm2014,BENOIT1967}---is a recurring extension of this theme.

\subsection{Economics, incentives, policy, adoption, and the human dimension}

A consistent frontier is that biophysical understanding alone is insufficient; economic evaluation, incentive design, policy reform, and farmer engagement are decisive for adoption. \textcite{Liu2021} call for advanced case-by-case cost analyses and inter-agency action to promote 4R implementation, \textcite{Yuan2022} for more data on the cost-effectiveness and ecological benefits of conservation practices, and \textcite{McLellan2015} for incorporating practice costs into future work. Continued positive incentives such as CREP funding for wetlands, more educational programmes to increase adoption, and greater consideration of non-excludable benefits in multifunctional landscapes are recommended \parencite{Christianson2014}, and \textcite{David2015} argue that incentivising on-farm research, generating a collective sense of conservation need, and scaling individual efforts to meso- and macro-scale policy are essential. Cost-share and risk-insurance mechanisms \parencite{Jaynes2012,Karlen2005}, legislative reassessment and revised incentives to remove barriers to natural-water-retention measures \parencite{Kvítek2023}, national policy change to retain open ditch-and-field networks with targeted agri-environmental support \parencite{Marja2013}, and nutrient-trading programmes for shallow-drain adoption \parencite{Ghane2021b} are all advanced.

Adoption, capacity, and social dimensions are stressed across contexts. Detailed cost-benefit analyses, joint farmer--researcher experimentation, and capacity building are prioritised, with adoption depending on farmers' long-term cost-benefit perspective \parencite{Okwany2016}; creating know-how and farmer confidence before project execution is emphasised \parencite{Ghumman2011}; and key practice-use issues---where to apply BMPs, tool availability, how to bundle, and transferability to commercial farms---are identified \parencite{Ward2018}. Stakeholder willingness to adopt must inform proposed measures \parencite{Cuevas2019}, and research integrating ecological, pollution-related, and economic impacts of alternative land uses, with models for optimal policy design, is urged \parencite{Saikkonen2014}. In salinity and irrigation contexts, packages spanning reduced rice plantings, farmer compensation, flexible cost-recovery, and gradual subsidy liberalisation \parencite{Kotb2000}, and analysis showing that feasible drainage-water recycling requires markedly higher, less volatile irrigated yields and lower adoption costs \parencite{Sellars2022} are advanced. Economics of cover crops \parencite{Waring2020}, quantification of soil-health and P-loss impacts to guide subsidies \parencite{Roth2018}, long-term cumulative yield-and-profit assessment of drainage installation \parencite{Mourtzinis2021}, producer-participation and pilot-programme information for policy optimisation \parencite{Malone2023}, integrated biophysical--economic modelling at field scale \parencite{Ferin2023}, replicable studies in the Lower Mississippi Alluvial Valley for policy analysis \parencite{Kröger2012}, and landscape-zone management with decision tools accounting for soil effects on yield \parencite{Bakhsh2007b,Nelson2011,Kanwar1997} together define the economic and policy dimension.

\subsection{Synthesis: converging priorities for the field}

Read together, these frontiers underscore a coherent research programme rather than a scattering of isolated gaps. The field repeatedly asks for the same foundational investments---longer, standardised, multi-site records that span climatic variability and slow soil evolution; richer metrics for phosphorus saturation, colloidal transport, and legacy nutrients; isotopic and tracer methods to resolve pathways and apportion sources; and process-based models whose weakest links (winter and frozen-soil processes, soil C--N cycling, macropore flow, and groundwater coupling) are strengthened and validated against better data. On the management side, the consistent message is that no single practice suffices: in-field nitrogen and cropping-system change, drainage-water and water-table management, and edge-of-field treatment must be bundled, sequenced, and spatially targeted to hydrologically active critical source areas, with explicit attention to tradeoffs among nitrate reduction, phosphorus loss, greenhouse-gas emissions, yield, and economic risk. Crucially, the biophysical questions are inseparable from human ones---cost-effectiveness, incentive design, policy reform, and farmer adoption---so that the decisive advances will come from interdisciplinary work linking mechanistic understanding, robust tools and data infrastructure, and economically and socially viable pathways to adoption \parencite{Ma2025,SKAGGS1994,Ferin2023,Kvítek2023,Baulch2019}.

2.body:
\subsection{Overview}
Edge-of-field denitrifying woodchip bioreactors and their companion treatment structures have matured to the point where discrete performance demonstrations are abundant, yet the field remains defined more by open questions than by settled design rules. Bioreactors remove nitrate from agricultural subsurface drainage through microbially mediated denitrification, but their performance is constrained by low water temperatures, variable hydraulic retention times (HRTs), and the biogeochemical evolution of the woodchip medium as it ages. Synthesising across the literature, the outstanding research frontiers cluster into several interlocking lines: (i) how to size, scale, and standardise bioreactor design---including for undersized systems and hydrologically flashy sites---and how to resolve the internal hydraulics and tracer methods on which design procedures rest; (ii) whether the microbial engine can be deliberately enhanced, for example through bioaugmentation with cold-adapted denitrifiers, and how to understand the resident microbiome that any such intervention must work with; (iii) how to select, amend, and manage reactive media---woodchips, corncobs, alternative carbon sources, envelopes, and phosphorus-sorbing materials---to extend performance and lifespan; (iv) how to constrain the pollution-swapping and unintended by-products (greenhouse gases, dissolved organic carbon, nitrite, phosphorus release, methylmercury) that accompany reductive treatment; (v) how contaminants---nitrate, phosphorus, pesticides, pathogens, antibiotic-resistance genes, and trace elements---are transported through drainage inlets, macropores, and riparian zones; and (vi) how longer-term, multi-site monitoring, broader metric reporting, and improved temperature-sensitive models can turn site-scale results into transferable design guidance. A recurring meta-message is the call for standardised design procedures, broader performance-metric reporting, and long-term data collected across climates, landscapes, and maturation stages so that watershed-scale nutrient-reduction goals can be met \parencite{Christianson2021,Maxwell2022,Bell2015}. The evidence synthesised below is drawn overwhelmingly from site-scale field and laboratory studies concentrated in the U.S.\ Midwest, with additional work from the Mid-Atlantic, Ohio, Virginia, North Carolina, Lithuania, Japan, and global syntheses; throughout, the emphasis is on what remains to be tested rather than what is already established.

\subsection{Sizing, Scaling, and Standardised Design Procedures}
A central frontier is the reconciliation of site-level effectiveness with watershed-scale load removal. \textcite{Christianson2026} argue that effort should be directed toward better balancing bioreactors that are as effective as practical at the site level while still removing as much nitrogen load as possible; they note it would be valuable to assess the performance of undersized bioreactors in larger drainage areas, suggest that greater flexibility in design processes may help scale the practice to those larger areas, and caution that additional empirical data are needed to strengthen the loading-density relationship and that performance data from full-size bioreactors beyond Illinois are required to refine prediction models before they are used for design. In parallel, \textcite{Dougherty2020b} propose that particularly flashy drainage sites could size up bioreactor designs to meet performance goals, and repeatedly call for simpler, standardised ``plug-and-play'' design approaches, positioning their flow analysis as a step toward a simplified design procedure. Standardisation is echoed at the practice-policy level by \textcite{Christianson2021}, who recommend refining the Conservation Practice Standard and streamlining the design process.

The adequacy of residence-time (T)-centric design is itself questioned. \textcite{deOliveira2023} advocate additional field-scale assessment of baffles in relatively wide bioreactors to increase hydraulic loading while maintaining hydraulic efficiency, call for more tracer testing at full-size bioreactors of varying designs coupled with nitrate monitoring and modelling, and specifically flag the weaknesses of current T-centric design approaches---including field-scale tracer testing of non-standard media---as a target for optimisation. Foundational design-procedure validation remains incomplete: \textcite{Christianson2013} call for further field-scale validation of the drainage-bioreactor design procedure against performance data from bioreactors of different designs, and \textcite{Ghane2015} recommend validating hydraulic models at additional denitrification-bed sites, using them to develop optimal design guidelines, and adopting additional conservative tracers such as isotopes to better characterise bed hydraulics. Region-specific parameterisation is also outstanding: \textcite{Povilaitis2020} note that flow rate, seasonality, microbial community, and other limiting factors must be evaluated to obtain reliable design parameters for Lithuania, and \textcite{Ghane2019} present bromide data intended to support further sorption experiments underpinning tracer-based design.

\subsection{Hydraulics, Hydraulic Retention Time, and Tracer Methods}
HRT is repeatedly identified as the master variable linking hydraulics to removal, yet its treatment in design guidance remains imperfect. \textcite{Moorman2015} state that increased research on the relationship between HRT and nitrate-nitrogen removal would improve bioreactor sizing criteria, while \textcite{Povilaitis2018} call for better optimisation and proper evaluation of HRT effects to improve woodchip-bioreactor design, and for more laboratory- and field-scale investigations to quantify long-term performance and optimise nitrate-nitrogen removal rates across flow conditions. In a global synthesis, \textcite{Christianson2025c} recommend several methodological advances: assessing varying HRT by changing flow rate versus sampling along the reactor length; increasing transparency in methods for estimating woodchip porosity; reporting dissolved oxygen, pH, and dissolved organic carbon (DOC) more consistently; using machine-learning approaches as data accumulate to refine HRT--removal-efficiency parameter estimates; and continuing work across all scales and bioreactor ages.

The internal hydraulics of woodchip media are themselves poorly resolved. \textcite{Ghane2016} argue that future research should exploit the non-Darcy flow characteristics of woodchips to adjust current NRCS design guidelines for denitrification beds and yield more efficient systems. Tracer methodology is a specific bottleneck: \textcite{Ghane2019b} raise concerns about low bromide recovery and recommend testing for organobromine in woodchips following bromide tracer tests, microscopic examination of interior woodchip pores to assess bromide entrapment, excavating a bed after tracer testing to analyse for physically trapped bromide, ascertaining the cause of low recovery, and either accounting for possible bromide retardation or using non-sorbing conservative tracers such as isotopes. \textcite{Ghane2018} likewise recommend tracer tests to determine differences in hydraulic performance of retrofitted beds so that beds can be compared in subsequent experiments. Together these point to an unresolved need for reliable tracer methods before hydraulic performance can be benchmarked.

\subsection{Bioaugmentation and Direct Microbial Manipulation}
The defining frontier for enhancing the microbial engine is whether denitrifying microorganisms can be inoculated into woodchip bioreactors to raise nitrate removal, especially where cold temperatures limit biological activity. \textcite{Jang2019} propose that inoculating denitrifiers (bioaugmentation) might increase removal at low-temperature conditions, but note that genome completion and further data mining are still necessary to identify the nitric oxide (NO) reductase of \emph{Cellulomonas} sp.\ strain WB94---an unresolved step in confirming a complete denitrification pathway for a candidate inoculant. \textcite{Anderson2020} frame the key unanswered questions explicitly: further research is needed to confirm the efficacy of inoculating these denitrifiers under field conditions, including whether inoculated strains actually enhance nitrogen removal in the field, how long they survive in bioreactors, and whether carbon addition can enhance the abundance of both inoculated and indigenous denitrifiers; the authors state they plan to address these questions. Survival and persistence of the inoculum thus emerge as first-order uncertainties, echoed at the global scale by \textcite{Wang2025b}, who call for field-scale bioaugmentation methods that minimise inoculum loss and ensure persistence of inoculated cultures, and for expanding knowledge of \emph{nod}-harbouring bacteria across agricultural settings.

A parallel line concerns the terminal steps of denitrification. \textcite{Aldossari2021} report that further research is necessary to confirm the reduction of nitrous oxide (N\textsubscript{2}O) to dinitrogen (N\textsubscript{2}) in fungal strains, including identification of nitrous oxide reductase genes on their genomes, and---critically---to see whether these strains can reduce nitrate to N\textsubscript{2} under woodchip bioreactor conditions; they add that confirmatory experiments are needed to examine the usefulness of the acetylene inhibition technique for fungal N\textsubscript{2}O reduction and that more defined media should be used to clarify nitrate-consuming pathways. Beyond conventional heterotrophic denitrifiers, \textcite{Wang2025b} identify emerging targets and strategies: bioaugmenting methanogenic environments with nitrate-dependent anaerobic methane oxidation (n-DAMO) and other oxygenic denitrifiers; providing suitable conditions as a biostimulation strategy to enhance the competitiveness of inoculated n-DAMO bacteria against other nitrogen-cycling processes; and exploring algal--bacterial granular sludge technology for agricultural drainage treatment---directions that extend bioaugmentation from single-strain inoculation toward engineered consortia and coupled metabolic guilds. Microbial-amendment strategies more broadly require scale-up testing: \textcite{Jeglot2022} recommend that bioaugmentation, possibly combined with biostimulation, be tested under replicated pilot-scale conditions to provide empirical and statistical evidence for upscaling to full-scale bioreactors, and \textcite{Ma2021} call for investigation of the inhibition and denitrifier-enhancement mechanisms caused by heated tourmaline and for field-scale tests of such bioremediation strategies.

Against this enthusiasm, \textcite{Hathaway2015} offer a counter-hypothesis worth testing directly: that optimisation of denitrifying bioreactors is more likely to result from refinements in design and management parameters influencing environmental conditions than from inoculation or other direct manipulations of the microbial community. Whether bioaugmentation can outperform---or must be combined with---such environmental engineering remains an open, testable question and a useful null hypothesis for future field trials.

\subsection{The Resident Microbiome and By-Product Formation}
Rational manipulation of bioreactor microbiology presupposes a mechanistic understanding of the resident community, which remains incomplete. \textcite{Hartfiel2022} argue that future work should draw on soil and wetland ecology to inform the study of bioreactor microbiomes, that the factors influencing cellulose and lignin metabolism genes and the community members that promote substrate degradation warrant further study, that dissimilatory nitrate reduction to ammonium (DNRA) and anammox have been observed in bioreactor systems and warrant study given limited attention to them, and that varying substrates to select for differing microbial communities is a promising avenue to promote consistency and potentially reduce by-product formation. The role of the microbial community in harmful by-product formation---including greenhouse-gas (GHG) production, sulfate reduction, and subsequent methylmercury production---also warrants study, and although HRT has been implicated as a design factor contributing to by-product production, the mechanism by which HRT impacts the community should be examined \parencite{Hartfiel2022}. Complementing this, \textcite{Bock2018} suggest that continued study of GHG emissions might enable a more precise comparison of the nitrate-nitrogen removal gained by increasing HRTs against the additional methane (CH\textsubscript{4}) produced, that managing bed pH may be important for acidic agricultural drainage to enhance nitrogen removal and mitigate N\textsubscript{2}O emissions, and that conceptualising how regional differences impact in-bed controls on nitrogen removal will guide adaptation of designs to a wider range of agroecosystems.

At the operational level, \textcite{Kouanda2021} recommend that nitrite be evaluated during operation to avoid high nitrite levels in reactor effluents---a specific pollution-swapping pathway needing routine monitoring. \textcite{Wang2025b} generalise the concern, urging that future efforts optimise carbon-utilisation efficiency to minimise pollution swapping and reduce carbon consumption by non-denitrifying processes, and that research clarify the long-term effects of conservation practices on the soil and sediment microbial communities responsible for nitrate remediation. Selective pressures on community assembly also merit dedicated study: \textcite{Schaefer2021} propose characterising the effect of oxygen load on microbial community composition in contrast to the selective pressure of nitrate load, and argue for further research on the microbial characteristics of denitrifying bioreactors to explore differences in decomposition and denitrification performance across bioreactors operating at different HRTs.

\subsection{Reactive Media, Carbon Sources, and Substrate Innovation}
Beyond conventional woodchips, several alternative fill media and carbon-management strategies remain incompletely characterised. \textcite{Law2023} encourage more field studies of corncobs as a bioreactor medium---including longer-term research capturing spatial and temporal variability for robust cost analysis, evaluation of the nitrogen-removal-rate range and whether a lower maximum HRT is needed, investigation of unintended pollution swapping such as nutrient and pesticide leaching, and assessment of GHG production and biophysical properties under different conditions. \textcite{Porter2015} call for research into organic-carbon availability at different depths while accounting for moisture content, noting that if a link between moisture, carbon availability, and performance can be established, future designs could be altered---for example by using different substrates or by raising or lowering internal water levels---and suggest extending short-term microbial-diversity studies over longer periods to reveal recurring annual patterns in alpha diversity.

Because woodchip carbon fuels denitrification, its release kinetics, biodegradability, and pre-treatment are active frontiers. \textcite{Kouanda2021} report that long-term laboratory and field studies are needed to evaluate the longevity of the effect of woodchip composting on nitrate removal, and that more studies are needed to determine the biodegradability of woodchip DOC and the release rates of biodegradable organic carbon to better understand nitrate-removal kinetics. Startup carbon losses raise both water-quality and downstream-treatment concerns: \textcite{Abusallout2017} suggest that the initial flush of woodchips may be recycled back to agricultural fields to reduce high organic-carbon loadings during startup, that pre-washing woodchips before installation could reduce the initial DOC-releasing potential, and that drinking-water treatment plants may need to adjust coagulant types and doses to remove woodchip-derived DOC and reduce disinfection by-product formation potential.

Carbon dosing and synthetic-carbon supplementation are active but unresolved. \textcite{Rivas2020} identify the specifics of adding readily available carbon---source, frequency, and amount---to optimise dissolved reactive phosphorus (DRP) and performance under highly dynamic inflow, while cautioning that more work is needed to minimise excess DOC in outflow and potential biofilm clogging when carbon dosing is used. \textcite{Zhang2021b} identify continued study of operational parameters (especially HRT), the hybrid application of synthetic carbon sources, the design of low-temperature-tolerant reactors, and the mixing of synthetic carbon sources for complex water bodies as worthwhile directions. Substrate selection is also proposed by \textcite{Hartfiel2022} as a lever to shape microbial communities and by-product outcomes.

\subsection{Envelopes, Filter Aggregates, and Structural Materials}
A distinct materials frontier concerns drain envelopes and structural fill. \textcite{Kaboosi2012} recommend evaluating envelopes with different combination ratios of rice husk, sand, and gravel and conducting field experiments under real conditions before drawing conclusions on rice husk as a drain envelope, while \textcite{Navabian2017} stress long-term field experiments to determine the environmental and technical function of a combined rice-husk-and-sand envelope, given the degradation potential of rice husk and its tendency to create acidic conditions in the envelope medium. For aggregate sizing, \textcite{Byrne2023} recommend that aggregates ranging from 0.7 to 19~mm be tested in situ in clay-textured soils, with the identified optimal subset subsequently field-tested. Synthetic envelopes require material-by-material evaluation: \textcite{Byrne2023b} note that needle-punched non-woven geotextile envelopes need further testing, that thicker synthetic envelopes with performance comparable to aggregates may be required, that each synthetic envelope must be tested individually because of varying physical properties, and that filter-sock-plus-aggregate combinations should not be recommended until further research is carried out. Structural concrete alternatives also remain untested at scale: \textcite{Wang2016c} call for research into the practical application and durability of fly-ash- and coal-gangue-based concrete under field conditions with high groundwater levels.

\subsection{Phosphorus-Removal Media, Filter Design, and Novel P Dynamics}
Phosphorus-sorption materials (PSMs) constitute one of the most heavily populated research-gap areas, spanning material selection, longevity, regeneration, and hydraulic integration. General selection criteria are laid out by \textcite{Li2020}, who argue that adsorbent choice must account for phosphorus concentration, interfering ions, temperature, flow rate, and sediment loads; prioritise selectivity for phosphorus at neutral pH; withstand seasonally fluctuating temperature, redox condition, flow rate, and sediment load without sacrificing removal efficiency; be testable for reusability and ease of recovery; and be economical for large field-scale application. \textcite{Mendes2022} add that the apparently synergistic retention mechanism and performance of filter media at high flow rates remain unknown, and that recycling and regeneration of spent filter media should be verified and pursued.

Iron- and steel-based media dominate the specific proposals. \textcite{Allred2017} call for optimising a modified iron-oxyhydroxide regeneration process using dilute hydrochloric acid followed by a NaOH flush, developing practices for collecting and treating the resulting acid/NaOH effluent, testing larger iron-oxyhydroxide systems capable of treating a greater percentage of total drainage flow, and evaluating low-cost goethite sources such as bog iron ore. \textcite{Allred2014} recommend more laboratory testing of zero-valent iron and related media to determine minimum contact time for phosphate removal, higher-flow-rate column tests, rigorous adsorption-isotherm tests to identify removal mechanisms, and field-scale filter treatment studies as a follow-on. \textcite{Hua2016} note that steel by-products may also remove methylmercury and may be regenerated by alkaline washing to recover adsorbed phosphate, while \textcite{Sellner2019b} suggest connecting a steel by-product filter to a woodchip bioreactor only after the initial bioreactor flush (e.g.\ 1--2 weeks) to minimise DOC interference, and mixing fine steel particles with other media to increase hydraulic conductivity and reduce clogging. \textcite{Wang2020d} call for optimising the relationship among removal efficiency, retention time, steel-slag mass, and cost when designing a P-removal structure, while \textcite{Shedekar2020} plan to replace spent slag with a lower-cost gravel--metal-shavings mixture, note that future structures should use a thicker slag bed and reduced length to improve uniform flow distribution, and emphasise controlling sediment influx---especially from bank erosion. \textcite{Penn2020} recommend PSMs that remove phosphorus via ligand exchange onto aluminium and iron oxide/hydroxide minerals (mine-drainage residuals, manufactured PSMs, aluminium-coated steel slag) and note that such materials suit tile-drainage treatment only where structures are designed for high-frequency media replacement---every 4--6 months in their study.

Fly-ash and pellet media raise saturation-timing questions. \textcite{Li2017} stress that saturation time must be quantified before field application so that fly-ash pellets can be replaced before they shift from phosphorus sink to source, and \textcite{Li2018b} direct follow-up research toward the hydraulic parameters of fly-ash pellets combined with recovery, and toward constraints such as poor saturation time, high outflow pH, and possible field desorption. Filter-media blends more broadly remain unoptimised: \textcite{Agrawal2011} call for determining the feasibility of filter installation and long-term field-scale performance, and \textcite{Agrawal2011b} report a need to determine the optimal media blend and configuration, to identify which constituent(s) offload phosphate and for how long before saturation, and to design larger or new filters capable of accommodating high drainage flows with sufficient contact time. Contact-time resolution itself is flagged by \textcite{Kumari2025}, who suggest exploring shorter contact-time intervals (e.g.\ 30~min) to capture the rapid initial adsorption phase, while anisotropic-medium behaviour warrants further study per \textcite{Kankarej2023}. Integrated woodchip-plus-PSM designs need multi-scale evaluation: \textcite{Husk2018} call for assessing mixed-media phosphorus-removal effectiveness under additional climate, soil, and cropping conditions over multiyear periods and at different scales to develop predictive design models, evaluating alternative or multiple media placements within bioreactors, examining effective P-removal lifespan and reuse/regeneration/recycling potential, and studying operational parameters such as pH, biochemical oxygen demand, and conductivity, including microbially mediated processes and removal of agrochemicals and microbial contaminants.

Emerging mechanisms of phosphorus interaction with woodchips remain to be elucidated. \textcite{Zhang2024b} call for focused experiments on the processes leading to enhanced orthophosphate uptake by woodchips undergoing anoxic--(anaerobic--)oxic cycling, phenotypic identification of polyphosphate-accumulating organisms in woodchip biofilms to confirm enhanced-biological-phosphorus-removal-like mechanisms, and determination of whether anaerobic conditions are necessary to promote the observed uptake. Redox-driven phosphorus release is a recurring monitoring gap: \textcite{Bailon2025} call for evaluating bioreactor start-up flushes with immediate monitoring, aging bioreactors, and total phosphorus alongside higher-frequency DRP monitoring, and for further assessment of redox-driven P release and the use of drawdown plates. \textcite{Rivas2020} likewise call for continued multi-season monitoring to confirm the longer-term DRP removal potential of woodchip bioreactors and further study of the dynamics and factors determining DRP removal. Passive-sampling monitoring itself needs development: \textcite{Li2020b} call for a more reactive hybrid resin adaptable to a wide range of temperature and flow rate, investigation of particulate-phosphorus effects on passive-sampling estimates, mesh-bag materials with reduced biofouling for long-term deployment, and extension of the technique to large-scale watershed and river-basin monitoring. Fundamental characterisation of dissolved organic phosphorus is sought by \textcite{Liu2024b}, who call for investigating the roles of the origin and management of organic matter and organic P on P lability and dissolved-organic-P leaching, and the bioavailability of dissolved organic phosphorus in recipient waters.

\subsection{Pollution Swapping, Greenhouse Gases, and Carbon Export}
Reductive treatment introduces trade-offs that are only partly quantified. \textcite{Christianson2021} name reducing pollution swapping---especially at start-up---and improving understanding of the removal of additional pollutants, compounds, and biologicals as key research areas. Greenhouse-gas emissions are a prominent concern: \textcite{Brunton2024} recommend further GHG research on bioreactors, fuller assessment of full-size bioreactor methane dynamics, examination of the impacts of bioreactor age and associated woodchip properties on GHG consumption and release, evaluation of ``hot moment'' GHG production, additional monitoring of methane production at new bioreactors, longer-term analyses paired with in situ woodchip physical and chemical property measurements, and further field-scale investigation of soil-cover benefits and trade-offs; they also suggest testing sophisticated automated control structures for real-time management based on outflow oxidation--reduction potential. \textcite{Strock2025} note that, because a 2-h HRT has been shown to increase GHG emissions, additional research on this design consequence is needed, along with work on the longevity of P-removal materials, optimisation of performance under cold conditions, monitoring of DOC export, and strategies for managing P-treatment materials to minimise unintended phosphorus release. \textcite{Maxwell2022} suggest that minimising negative by-products such as mercury methylation could be based on an outflow nitrate-concentration design criterion rather than a mass metric. Nitrite and long-term P transport are flagged by \textcite{Bell2015}, who call for measuring nitrite concentrations in future bioreactor studies and for long-term evaluation of phosphorus transport from bioreactors. Carbon-export accounting more broadly is a monitoring gap: \textcite{Schilling2023} call for improved monitoring of aqueous total-carbon export for accurate carbon-sequestration accounting, multiple years of data to characterise carbon export under variable climate, and note that reducing water throughput via water-level control structures may minimise dissolved-inorganic-carbon loss.

\subsection{Aging, Longevity, Clogging, Subsidence, and Construction}
The multi-decadal service life and physical stability of treatment structures are among the least-studied dimensions and a clear research priority. \textcite{Ghane2018} recommend continuing investigation of the reduction of woodchip particle size over a longer period because particle size affects water flow through denitrification beds; investigating whether woodchip carbon becomes recalcitrant to the point of nitrate-removal inefficiency before particle-size reduction affects flow; and conducting longer-term studies beyond ten years to understand how age affects woodchip carbon properties and removal performance. \textcite{Christianson2020} similarly call for long-term studies documenting biofilm formation and flushing, siltation, wood decay, and additional water-chemistry parameters across the life of a bioreactor to inform threshold values for woodchip recharge and guide assessment of aging bioreactors, together with additional research on the effects on denitrification performance of decreasing carbon-to-nitrogen (C:N) ratio and increasing lignocellulose index over time. Long-term field programmes to establish efficacy are ongoing, with \textcite{Greenan2009} noting that long-term ($>$8~yr) field studies were underway to assess the efficacy of woodchip denitrification bioreactors. Spatiotemporal monitoring under variable hydrology is also flagged: \textcite{Hartfiel2023b} recommend evaluating spatiotemporal nitrate-nitrogen removal under normal, wet, and drought years and across longer time periods, and combining spatiotemporal nitrate monitoring with woodchip C:N measurements to identify strategies to improve design.

Estimating replacement interval and cost depends on key unknowns; \textcite{Kohn2023} state that additional research is needed before widespread implementation, that bioreactor-function longevity is an important consideration, that estimating replacement interval and cost depends on performance, lifespan, and actual producer cost of agricultural residues, and that using a pre-measured feedstock volume per bioreactor with consistent placement could mitigate construction-method effects. Chemical clogging is mechanistically unresolved: \textcite{Guo2020} call for exploring the mechanism of chemical clogging and developing simulation models for technical prevention, a need echoed by \textcite{Guo2021}. Subsidence monitoring is a new methodological frontier: \textcite{Christianson2025b} suggest applying their LiDAR method at additional sites---especially following construction---to build subsidence time series, addressing whether subsidence is faster immediately after construction, collecting 3D surface data immediately post-construction, and assessing the practicality and benefit of targeted recharge near the inlet. Sediment management at construction is a design frontier for catchment-scale systems: \textcite{Feyereisen2023} plan to investigate the opposing effects of bypass flow (decreasing removal) and nitrate removal rate (increasing removal) in a sediment-exclusion system so managers can understand the net impact, recommend installing data-logging pressure transducers in each bed from project outset, and advise a pre-construction investigation of sedimentation sources with sediment-prevention measures included in design. Network-scale diagnostic methods also need extension: \textcite{Henine2021} recommend using an air compressor with higher injection pressure and airflow to extend a diagnostic method to much larger subsurface-drainage networks, monitoring electrical-resistivity variations with time-domain frequency electromagnetic methods and precise geolocation, and developing a safe air-injection system operable directly from the drain outlet.

\subsection{Pumped, Energy-Assisted, and Coupled Treatment Configurations}
Pumping to increase hydraulic loading is an emerging but incompletely validated design avenue. \textcite{DíazGarcía2025} call for additional assessment of different pumping configurations and bioreactor designs to contextualise benefits and drawbacks, note that small but consistent post-batching temperature increases merit further study, observe that a power-storage unit could have increased flow treatment, identify operating pumps continuously for 24~h and doubling the pumping window to six months as hypothetical ways to increase hydraulic loading, and caution that pumping should perhaps be considered only in the most suitable applications. \textcite{Hartfiel2024} call for exploring solar-powered pumped bioreactors to reduce operating and unit costs, verifying pumped-bioreactor performance before policy implications can be realised, and optimising system performance while reducing unit cost. Building on this, \textcite{Hartfiel2023} advance recommendations for pumped systems: using the actual bioreactor system capacity rather than the design capacity for sizing future pumping systems; timing pumping to consider source-water nitrate concentration, temperature, and carbon content; avoiding pumping during periods of high temperatures and/or carbon levels with lower nitrate concentrations; and extending pumped applications to nitrate-laden surface waters, irrigation waters, drainage ditches, pumped cistern systems for stored subsurface drainage, or groundwater. \textcite{Hartfiel2023b} propose additional management experiments: evaluating partial bioreactor-recharge strategies near the inlet and their long-term effects; developing similar management recommendations for bioreactors treating alternative nitrate-laden water sources; and managing dual-chamber bioreactors so that only one parallel chamber is used under traditional or low-flow conditions while the second is engaged under higher-flow conditions. Pumped constructed-wetland-plus-woodchip-bioreactor systems similarly require further work: \textcite{Pugliese2020} call for testing that analyses the concentration and proportion of particulate and dissolved nutrient forms during highly variable, intense discharges, a cost-effectiveness analysis of the synergistic surface-flow-wetland-plus-woodchip-bioreactor combination versus standalone systems, and longer monitoring to define the pumping effect on total-phosphorus retention.

Novel source waters raise their own questions. \textcite{Easton2019} note that additional research is needed to evaluate how denitrification rates respond to the flow and concentration conditions unique to springs under field conditions, and that water-policy changes---including financial incentives based on payments for quantities of nitrogen removed---may be needed to incentivise landowner adoption of spring bioreactors and stimulate investment in treating legacy nitrogen. Beyond passive woodchip beds, several enhanced configurations define emerging directions. \textcite{Law2018} recommend testing the performance of a well-designed bioelectrochemical reactor under low-temperature conditions and propose specific design refinements---a horizontal-flow reactor with an upstream anode, baffles to separate oxidising and reducing zones, and plate-shaped cathodes---together with lower current intensity and larger cathode surface area as key changes to reduce nitrate-removal cost and improve performance. For co-treatment of phosphorus, \textcite{Penn2024} recommend constructing phosphorus-removal structures at phosphorus critical source areas for maximum efficiency, designing bioreactors and phosphorus-removal structures to treat as much peak flow as possible, and pursuing further research to confirm or reject the proposed redox-coupled Fe(II)--nitrate reaction for nitrate removal by activated aluminium. These coupled systems broaden the design space in which enhanced denitrification---whether biological or electrochemical---might be pursued.

\subsection{Contaminant Transport Through Drainage Inlets and Macropores}
Understanding how contaminants enter and move through drainage networks is explicitly under-studied. \textcite{Li2017b} recommend additional research on how nitrate, phosphorus, and pesticides are transported through drainage inlets, studies of inlet performance under different debris from different crop systems, and modification of the flow equations to represent actual inlet performance under debris conditions. The definition of a bioreactor's effective contributing area is itself uncertain: \textcite{Akay2008} question whether the contributing area should vary for water, tracers, and reactive contaminants. Sediment behaviour within tiles is likewise unresolved: \textcite{Stone1997} note that the relative importance of bacteria versus solution-chemistry changes on flocculation of tile sediment across a range of turbulent shear stresses remains to be determined.

Pesticide transport modelling forms a coherent cluster of gaps. \textcite{Wendell2024} propose implementing algorithms describing particle-mediated colloidal transport, delineating upland areas and floodplains together with pesticide accumulation when surface runoff infiltrates or evaporates, separately accounting for macropore flow when calculating pesticide leaching, and continuous improvements in modelling and observation to reduce uncertainty in pesticide degradation and transport predictions. \textcite{Zehe2001} argue that convection--dispersion or transfer-function process models must be improved to include pesticide transport by mobile particles and that application guidelines should include soil-specific interactions. \textcite{Fox2007b} identify the need for a quasi-two-dimensional procedure to distribute pesticide across the soil profile (rather than the current one-dimensional approach), site-specific calibration of the enrichment-factor parameter, more sophisticated accounting for macropore/subsurface-drain interconnectivity in chemical routing, and user awareness of model limitations. \textcite{Novak2003} call for better field-scale evaluation of metolachlor retention describing slow immobilisation, and for apparent-dissociation-kinetics processes to be incorporated in registration and management models, especially for persistent pesticides. \textcite{Dousset2004} argue that field-scale monitoring of herbicide losses in drains is necessary to compare molecule behaviour across soil types over at least two planting periods to reliably assess leaching risk, and \textcite{Leu2004} call for more quantitative functions relating available field characteristics to a field's vulnerability to lose herbicides. Analytical and marker methods also need development: \textcite{MUIR1978} note that persistence of triazine degradation products could be modelled under better-controlled conditions and that methods for determining hydroxy-triazines in water are needed for routine monitoring, while \textcite{Heaven2012} suggest 3,5-dichloro-2-methoxybenzoic acid could serve as a chemical marker of dicamba pollution and stress identifying contaminant sources. Continued monitoring is advised by \textcite{Gordon2005} given widespread linuron use in carrot and potato production. Over-winter conditions are singled out by \textcite{Siimes2006}, who call for detailed study of soil freezing/thawing and snowmelt effects on herbicide fate and note their dataset will test pesticide-fate models in over-winter conditions.

\subsection{Pathogens, Microbial Ecology, and Antibiotic Resistance}
Microbial contaminant transport and treatment span several open questions. \textcite{Soupir2018} call for investigation of pathogen removal under lower HRTs, examination of both \textit{Salmonella} and \textit{E.\ coli} removal at lower HRT values, exploration of the removal mechanism to inform longevity, and investigation of microbial removal at higher flows. The ecology of indicator bacteria in treatment wetlands is unresolved: \textcite{Stott2023} call for determining the relative survival and growth of faecal \textit{E.\ coli} strains versus non-faecal ``\textit{E.\ coli}-like'' \textit{Escherichia} (cryptic clades) to understand wetlands as sources of \textit{E.\ coli} and downstream health risks, and to confirm or reject whether wetland and aquatic habitats support extended persistence and growth. \textcite{Truhlar2015} ask whether curli expression responds to manure-application method and call for characterising downstream risks associated with curli-producing versus non-producing \textit{E.\ coli}, especially regarding foodborne illness. Long-term pathogen leaching following manure application needs study: \textcite{Malik2004} call for additional temporal and long-term studies on the survival and leaching of pathogens and indicators into subsurface water before firm recommendations can be made for swine-manure application. Fecal-source tracking methods require corroboration: \textcite{Gottschall2013} recommend using other markers---such as pharmaceuticals and personal-care products and fecal indicator bacteria---in tandem with sterols, and using multiple sterol ratios for cross-validation. Irrigation-related pathogen risks lead \textcite{Ding2021} to strongly recommend disinfection of wastewater effluents used for irrigation, lower winter irrigation rates to slow pathogen movement through wet, cold soil, and year-round supplemental disinfection. Broader catchment-scale microbial priorities are set out by \textcite{Collins2007}, including quantifying off-stream water, shade, and shelter sources for cattle; understanding cattle attraction to waterways to prioritise fencing; experimental studies across soils, slopes, and vegetation to improve understanding of microbial entrapment in riparian buffers; evaluating constructed wetlands, vegetated ditches, and farm ponds for treating microbes in stormwater and effluent; assessing the costs of advocated mitigation measures; better understanding water as a pathogen vector for human infection; tracing pathogen strains causing cattle re-infection; and examining links between cattle productivity and drinking-water microbial quality.

Antibiotic resistance genes (ARGs) are an explicitly emerging frontier. \textcite{Smith2019b} call for better understanding of the diversity of ARGs, their mechanisms of persistence and dissemination, and long-term environmental effects, and recommend targeted approaches such as qPCR to complement metagenomic characterisation, while \textcite{Rieke2018} note that additional studies are needed to link the presence of resistance genes with host identification and transfer mechanisms.

\subsection{Companion Practices: Saturated Buffers, Wetlands, and Controlled Structures}
Frontier questions extend to practices deployed alongside or instead of bioreactors. For saturated buffers, \textcite{Ghane2025} recommend determining actual performance using a paired-field approach, further economic analysis of the buffer-flow component's cost-effectiveness, and determining average nitrate-load removal by the controlled-drainage and buffer-flow components under varying conditions, while \textcite{Jaynes2019b} highlight the need to establish and monitor additional saturated buffers with multipipe configurations. Treatment-wetland performance limits are a persistent theme: \textcite{Tanner2005} call for longer-term studies of sustainable nutrient-removal rates across climatic conditions, process-based laboratory and mesocosm investigations to supplement field studies, determination of whether organic-carbon availability limits denitrification and whether organic-matter supplements or strategic biomass cutting increase removal, evaluation of different flow regimes using hydraulic residence-time distributions and dynamic water-mass-balance models, and further information on attenuation of other contaminants (including pathogens), net GHG emissions, and biodiversity effects. \textcite{Tanner2003} recommend continued monitoring across seasons and years, intensive piezometer monitoring to quantify nutrient mass flows, studies of component processes to identify factors limiting nutrient removal, and supplementation of organic-carbon substrates with slow-release materials such as sawdust or straw. \textcite{Tanner2005b} note that further measurement and modelling are needed to estimate the frequency and composition of additional inflow sources, that performance might be optimised through different plant communities, soil types, organic-carbon amendments, or subsurface-flow zones, that longer-term studies are required to tease out event-to-event and year-to-year loading effects, and that larger wetland areas and/or enhanced areal denitrification rates would be needed to substantially reduce nitrogen export from pulsed drainage flows. Land-treatment wetland systems (FILTER) require subtropical adaptation per \textcite{Gardner2001}, who intend to increase applied-effluent nitrogen to representative values (about 25--30~mg~L\textsuperscript{-1} total nitrogen) and to clarify pathogen behaviour by tracking bacteriophage MS-2 after spiking.

Controlled and flow-regulating structures also have open questions. \textcite{Popek2021} recommend further research on improving the connection of regulator elements to limit effective flow sizes, and \textcite{Schilling2023} note that water-level control structures may reduce dissolved-inorganic-carbon loss. Water and sediment control basins warrant study of pipe-riser type effects on nutrient and sediment transport, event-scale sediment and phosphorus trapping within ponded areas, berm-area-plus-pipe-riser interactions, and the impact of directly routing larger sub-basins to the watershed outlet \parencite{Gupta2019}. Nutrient-loss management and drainage-inlet flow control are further addressed by \textcite{Nédélec2010}, who note the need for additional work on soil-surface clogging, storage-area geometry, and internal-drain discharge capacity, experimental validation of flow, sediment, and pollution-mitigation efficiency, and study of detrimental effects such as bio- or soil accumulation of pollutants.

\subsection{Riparian, Hyporheic, and Effluent-Management Processes}
Subsurface attenuation processes adjacent to drainage require broader characterisation. \textcite{Puckett2005} call for more intensive study of hyporheic-zone processes at the study site, establishment of sufficiently wide buffer zones to guarantee adequate residence times, and further work on hydrogeologic controls on nitrate transport through riparian zones across a wider range of settings. Effluent- and manure-management gaps include \textcite{Monaghan2007}, whose ongoing work indicates low-rate effluent irrigation can reduce direct transfers of effluent phosphorus and bacteria through mole-pipe drainage by up to 95\%, and \textcite{Gordon2005}, who call for research on the microbiological safety of liquid-hog-manure practices and long-term ($>$10~yr) field trials with repeated manure applications to determine water-quality impacts relative to standard fertility practices. Nutrient-application optimisation is targeted by \textcite{Farahani2020}, who call for determining nitrogen and phosphorus application rates and timings to reduce concentrations, and by \textcite{Singh2002}, who recommend splitting a 60~kg~N~ha\textsuperscript{-1} basal dose into three 20~kg~N~ha\textsuperscript{-1} doses at 10-day intervals. Tillage and soil-biology effects on phosphorus leaching remain open: \textcite{Ulén2018} call for further monitoring to confirm any trend of reduced efficiency, long-term studies of untilled and shallow-tillage plots, investigation of biological factors such as earthworm populations, and long-term monitoring to quantify phosphorus-leaching effects, particularly for fields treated in several ways.

\subsection{Trace-Element Legacies and Ecotoxicology}
Trace-element contamination associated with agricultural drainage in the western United States remains a long-horizon research and monitoring commitment. \textcite{BENSON1993} outline continued biological and groundwater monitoring, continued research to identify long-term (10--20~year) strategies for selenium dissipation and removal from surface soils, annual site-management planning submitted to the regulatory board, a pilot-scale demonstration of microbial volatilisation in a former cattail area using a 0.8~ha test plot with irrigation and tillage, and evaluation of a no-action management alternative. \textcite{SAIKI1987} note that additional studies are planned or underway to determine whether fish and other aquatic animals elsewhere in the San Joaquin Valley are also contaminated or adversely affected by selenium. Ecotoxicological monitoring of other agricultural contaminants is likewise recommended: \textcite{Campbell2023} call for investigating the mechanisms behind decreased cerebellum size, a more comprehensive analysis of predation, competition, and other ecological factors at field-collection sites, further examination of the relationship between imidacloprid and imidacloprid-olefin brain levels under field conditions, and regular monitoring of neonicotinoid levels in waterways near agricultural activity with tighter environmental restrictions to protect vulnerable non-target organisms such as amphibians. \textcite{Dreyfuss2018} note that findings for \textit{Galba truncatula} require reviewing the biogeographical status of the species and considering conservation measures given its role as intermediate host in the \textit{Fasciola hepatica} cycle.

\subsection{Cross-Cutting Gaps and Methodological Priorities}
Several open questions cut across the theme and constrain progress on the others. First, long-term and multi-site data recur regardless of practice: \textcite{Bell2015} call for more long-term studies across a variety of landscapes and more field-scale performance data from bioreactors of different designs and multiple Midwest locations; \textcite{Christianson2021} call for more long-term studies to advance bioreactor technologies; and \textcite{Bailon2025} recommend additional monitoring of a variety of bioreactor designs under different climates as treatment systems mature. Second, standardised and expanded performance-metric reporting: \textcite{Maxwell2022} argue that broadly reporting a variety of performance metrics will help scale both achievable treatment and implementation in non-point-source applications. Third, temperature-sensitive and mechanistic models: \textcite{Christianson2021} note that nitrate-removal-rate analysis across regional or seasonal temperature gradients would provide added insight and that recent bioreactor models should incorporate temperature sensitivity.

Beyond bioreactor-specific work, mechanistic field studies and improved in-situ measurement anchor the broader agenda. The mechanisms of nitrate export from agricultural fields and of denitrification in woodchip bioreactors require further investigation to generate practical guidelines, more events of different durations are needed for robust analysis of the influence of antecedent conditions on hysteresis patterns, and the legacy effects of animal-waste application and its interaction with plant growth, temperature dynamics, and drying--wetting periods warrant study \parencite{Liu2020}. The fate of organic chemicals is also unresolved: removal of organic chemicals by woodchip bioreactors needs to be investigated under field conditions \parencite{Ilhan2011}. Complementary in-situ interception methods warrant testing, as \textcite{Pampolino2000} suggest that installing a sufficient number of resin capsules in situ (for example, 10 capsules per 135~m\textsuperscript{2}) may allow interception and adsorption of a representative amount of nitrate transported via macropore or even bypass flow. Finally, at the broadest scale, effective management of agricultural subsurface drainage will likely depend on integrating multiple conservation practices to achieve targeted nitrate-reduction goals, and future studies should clarify the effects of controlled drainage on the denitrifying microbiome and the variables affecting its performance as a nitrogen-mitigation method \parencite{Wang2025b}. Together, these cross-cutting needs frame the overarching research agenda---moving from site-scale demonstration toward validated, transferable design guidance capable of meeting watershed-scale water-quality goals across the diverse climates, soils, and hydrologic regimes in which subsurface drainage operates.

3.body:
\subsection{Overview}

A recurring message across the subsurface (tile) drainage literature is that the current evidence base remains provisional. Many headline results derive from short records, single sites, or narrow agro-ecological conditions, and their authors explicitly caution against premature generalisation. The dominant frontier signal is therefore not a settled conclusion but a research agenda: the field is asked to extend monitoring in time, replicate it across space, validate models and emerging technologies at field scale, harmonise methods so that accumulating data can be synthesised, deepen mechanistic understanding of transport pathways and process interactions, and connect environmental endpoints to agronomic, economic, and social outcomes and to decision-making. Several authors pitch these needs at the level of the whole discipline. \textcite{Rogger2017}, for example, argue for complex-systems thinking that links processes across time scales, long-term experiments on physical--chemical--biological process interactions, a focus on connectivity and patterns across spatial scales, typologies and meta-analyses that generalise findings, multidisciplinary consortia with shared data and terminology, and long-term observation under controlled or known boundary conditions wherever possible. This section organises the specific monitoring and research needs identified across the reviewed studies along temporal, spatial, methodological, mechanistic, integrative, and programmatic dimensions.

The scale of the outstanding uncertainty is captured by the sheer frequency of direct appeals for more work. Studies conclude, variously, that ``more research is needed'' and that ``more verification would be beneficial'' \parencite{SHALIT1995}, that ``the complexity of this subject requires more research'' \parencite{Malone2014}, that ``further research in this area is needed'' \parencite{Woo2023}, and that ``further evaluations are warranted before any concrete conclusions could be drawn'' \parencite{GUPTA1993}. These are less rhetorical closings than signposts to genuine gaps in evidence, method, and synthesis, which the subsections below make explicit.

\subsection{Extending the Temporal Record}

\subsubsection{The Case for Long-Term, Multi-Year Monitoring}

The single most widely voiced need is for records long enough to separate genuine treatment effects from the natural variability of weather, hydrology, and crop growth. \textcite{Clifford2023} emphasise the need for truly long-term experiments and monitoring to assess management impacts accurately, and \textcite{BUHLER1993} caution that a lag of several years may be necessary before the effect of an altered practice can be objectively evaluated. \textcite{Ali2013} judge that longer monitoring periods---at least five years---and continued data collection are desirable, warning that long-term consequences may require continued observation if drainage systems are expanded in length or depth over time. In Egypt, \textcite{Ali2001} report that monitoring will continue and that longer time series for the whole country, expected within a few years, should enable more detailed analysis as the records lengthen.

At the site scale, many studies attach their conclusions to explicit requirements for additional years of observation. \textcite{Jaynes2008} note that continued monitoring of a denitrification wall over additional years is necessary to quantify its effectiveness against natural variability, to establish long-term cost per unit of nitrate removed, and to gauge its potential as a management tool. \textcite{Dalhem2019} expect continued sampling to reveal how long the positive effects of their treatments persist, and \textcite{Osterholm2005} judge it worthwhile to monitor a test site for several more years to assess the functionality of a combined technique. Similar calls for verification over longer periods come from \textcite{ROLLIN1987}, who plan observations spanning a longer service life; \textcite{Wesström2015}; \textcite{Reinhart2020}, who seek more widespread and longer-duration evaluations of a practice's potential impact; \textcite{Rijal2012}, who stress the need to assess the replicability of results in time; and \textcite{GiménezValero2025}, who conclude that additional multi-year measurements are needed to confirm whether observed differences persist under comparable conditions. Underlying all of these is the disciplinary argument of \textcite{SPALING1995} for long-term monitoring of environmental change at various scales.

\subsubsection{Temporal Resolution and Seasonal Coverage}

Record length is necessary but not sufficient: several authors highlight gaps in temporal \emph{resolution} and seasonal coverage. \textcite{Klaiber2020} call for additional years of monitoring together with improved, year-round measurement---especially during the non-growing season---to understand seasonal phosphorus exports and reach more robust conclusions. \textcite{Hodaj2017} stress avoiding gaps in high-frequency records, advising that future studies check instruments as frequently as possible. \textcite{Wesström2015} add a timing consideration specific to infrastructure interventions, noting that it is more appropriate to monitor nutrient leaching after drain repair.

\subsection{Broadening Spatial and Geographic Scope}

A second major frontier is testing whether site-specific findings hold across other locations, soils, climates, and management regimes. \textcite{Menegaz2025} caution that additional studies are needed before broad adoption of their results can be encouraged beyond local conditions, and \textcite{Zimmerman2017b} call for similar data from other sites and years. \textcite{Ali2001} plan to collect data from further sample areas reflecting Egypt's diverse agro-ecological conditions. \textcite{Hodaj2017} argue that exploring the two-stage ditch in other settings would benefit the practice, and \textcite{Bigelow2001} recommend research into effects where putting greens are severely compacted, have low percolation rates, or are maintained in unfavourable environments such as shade or excessive wetness, while suggesting the technology may have merit under other situations. \textcite{Terrado2015} likewise conclude that further monitoring and research in support of their findings is warranted. At the level of evidence synthesis, \textcite{Wu2025} argue that future syntheses require more robust, geographically diverse, and methodologically harmonised studies, and that a broader experimental base will let future meta-analyses better disentangle management effects.

\subsection{Field-Scale Validation of Models and Emerging Technologies}

Much reviewed work rests on models, pilot studies, or laboratory-scale results whose authors defer confident application until field-scale validation is available. \textcite{Mitchell2025} state plainly that ``field-scale research is needed to validate these results'' and that ``further research to validate our models is needed before they are widely applied.'' \textcite{Kalcic2018} similarly conclude that continued efforts to validate practice effectiveness at field and reach scales will be needed to verify whether models are correct, marking these as areas ripe for future development. \textcite{Garmdareh2018} recommend more rigorous testing of a model before its extensive use, and \textcite{Yu2016} call for validating their conclusions with more experiments. \textcite{SHALIT1995} identify a specific target, arguing that further investigation should determine how well the leaching-fraction model predicts concentrations arising from preferential flow. For emerging technologies, \textcite{DarziNaftchali2018b} stress that environmental effects must be quantified at the pilot-area scale before large-scale implementation, and \textcite{GUPTA1993} require further measurements and evaluations before concrete conclusions can be drawn.

\subsection{Monitoring Infrastructure, Instrumentation, and Data Tools}

A distinct set of needs concerns the practical apparatus of monitoring---the samplers, loggers, diagnostic methods, and software that make long-term data collection feasible and reliable. \textcite{Kanwar1999} describe how automatic samplers can collect discrete water samples alongside continuous ones, how additional equipment can be connected to data loggers to measure soil temperature or moisture, and how relays or pressure switches could replace electronic transmitters to detect sump-pump operation; they advise minimising the distance between equipment and loggers, downloading data automatically to reduce loss, and performing annual maintenance including cleaning check valves and calibrating flowmeters. Diagnostic methods are themselves a research target: \textcite{Petersen2012} propose reducing pressure variations by doubling the equipment and injecting smoke from both ends of a drain section---potentially enabling work with longer drain lines---while noting that further study is needed to resolve transport pathways and the reasons for observed clustering. \textcite{MartínezBeltrán2007} recommend observing selected pipes to determine the state of the envelope and perforations, observing the surrounding soil for its influence on radial flow in the trench, and permanently monitoring the sampling process to verify that favourable results are maintained over time.

Software and data infrastructure form an increasingly prominent part of this frontier. \textcite{Shedekar2018} advise readers to explore data products and tools further, to consult the National Elevation Dataset and USGS resources for the latest availability, and to track ongoing developments, while arguing that improved drainage-management practices are needed to increase adaptation to a changing climate. A series of tool-development efforts describe themselves as explicitly ongoing and open to community input: \textcite{Falasy2025} note continuing development---spanning topologically sound, one-way-flow tile networks and application tools for configuring the burying and sizing of drainage networks---and welcome testing with additional datasets; \textcite{Falasy2025b} report ongoing revision based on test-user feedback and encourage application beyond the samples provided; and \textcite{Falasy2024} state that development of their tools will be continual and invite testing with other data.

\subsection{Standardisation, Harmonisation, and Comparability}

For the accumulating body of monitoring data to support robust synthesis, several authors identify a need for standardised, harmonised methods. \textcite{Wu2025} tie the value of future meta-analyses to methodologically harmonised studies and to comprehensive, long-term monitoring of the full suite of environmental impacts. \textcite{Zhang2018} set out a detailed programme for environmental indicators: improving the quality and comparability of existing indicators through harmonised data collection, monitoring, interpretation, and reporting; developing new indicators for emerging environmental and policy concerns while ensuring scientific credibility, sensitivity to change, and ease of interpretation; establishing standardised evaluation procedures, including standards, thresholds, reference points, and complementary validation information; recognising data gaps and improving methods for upscaling, uncertainty assessment, and analysis of contradictory indicators; and adapting environmental models for indicator modelling while incorporating earth-observation data and reducing barriers to remote-sensing access. These priorities complement the call by \textcite{Rogger2017} for typologies, meta-analyses, and shared data and terminology across research consortia.

\subsection{Deepening Mechanistic Understanding}

A further frontier concerns mechanistic understanding: identifying the pathways, relationships, and process interactions that current data only partially resolve. \textcite{Malone2014} conclude that the complexity of the subject requires more research, and \textcite{Cui2016} state that more research is needed to define the relationships they observed. \textcite{Petersen2012} identify unresolved transport pathways and clustering patterns requiring further investigation. \textcite{Tomer2008} intend to pursue their central idea as a research avenue, to undertake bacterial source tracking within their watershed, and note that a specific transport pathway remains a subject for future research. \textcite{Ali2013} call for a separate geochemical modelling study to test whether geochemical reactions bind free heavy metals to the solid matrix. Process-level questions extend to plant response: \textcite{GiménezValero2025} note that an observation consistent with the role of phosphorus in flowering merits further study under controlled conditions, and \textcite{Tamura2021} report that further experiments are required to clarify a specific point. \textcite{LAL1993} indicate that plant-nutrient results will appear in a companion report and that a long-term experiment is being analysed to test their hypotheses. These needs echo the overarching argument of \textcite{Rogger2017} for long-term experiments on physical--chemical--biological process interactions and for attention to connectivity and patterns across spatial scales.

\subsection{Integrating Environmental, Agronomic, Economic, and Social Evidence}

Several authors argue that monitoring must move beyond environmental endpoints to encompass agronomic, economic, and social dimensions and to connect with decision-making. \textcite{Zhang2018} call for improved linkage of environmental indicators with those tracking economic and social change, in terms of both causes and effects. \textcite{Zhu2025} require further assessment of actual growth in conjunction with economic, social, and environmental factors, and \textcite{Brikmans2025} argue that future work should elucidate strategies for prolonging physical benefits while critically assessing the agronomic and economic feasibility of such approaches. \textcite{Monaghan2016} identify a need for research on the practical implications of management changes---in their case, keeping cows off pasture for extended periods. At the science--policy interface, \textcite{Letey1999} argue that plans should be updated as monitoring, additional studies, and local actions reveal new facts, and that the scientific community should evaluate its role in policymaking and focus research on questions of greatest value to decision makers as well as to scientific peers. \textcite{Clifford2023} call for scientific assessment and prioritisation of which approaches have the largest effects under what circumstances, alongside truly long-term experiments. \textcite{Kalcic2018} likewise argue that further work is needed to assess current knowledge and prioritise knowledge gaps, and that clear messaging through education and extension will be imperative to achieve implementation at the scales needed to address water-quality needs in the Mississippi River Basin and Great Lakes regions.

\subsection{Ongoing and Planned Monitoring Programmes}

Finally, the literature documents monitoring programmes that are explicitly ongoing or planned, underscoring that many conclusions are interim by design. \textcite{Tournebize2012} report that a performance-assessment programme was established in 2011 to determine whether co-constructed wetlands comply with new regulations, that intensive hydrological monitoring will continue, and that resizing will be considered if needed. \textcite{Kjaersgaard2016} note that monitoring was to begin in spring 2016, and \textcite{Ali2001} confirm that monitoring will continue. \textcite{Ohlendorf2002} describe how monitoring data were used to identify needs for contingency plans and to recommend further research and monitoring to improve site management, with additional studies subsequently conducted to resolve unknowns flagged by a risk assessment. Other authors signal forthcoming results: \textcite{Drury2014} indicate that data from remaining treatments will appear in a subsequent paper. Further calls for continued or expanded empirical effort come from \textcite{Rekha2011}, who state that more treatment studies are required, and from \textcite{Ogino2007}, who hope that research and development in the field will be actively promoted. Collectively, these programmes and appeals reinforce the central frontier message: robust, generalisable conclusions about subsurface drainage await the longer, broader, better-harmonised, and more integrative evidence base that the field is actively working to build.

4.body:
\subsection{Overview}

Across the tile-drainage modelling literature, a striking regularity is that the credibility of both process-based and empirical models is constrained less by their formulation than by the narrowness of the conditions under which they have been tested. Individual studies are typically calibrated at a single experimental site, over a handful of seasons, and for one soil--climate--management combination, and their authors are nearly unanimous in flagging broader empirical validation as the outstanding priority. Read together, these caveats are not incidental disclaimers but a shared research agenda. Calls to ``verify or modify coefficients when the model is applied to a new site'' \parencite{Li2006}, to test performance ``at other sites'' \parencite{Qi2015}, and to confirm simulations with dedicated field studies \parencite{Shipitalo2016,Yousfi2014} recur so consistently that they define, collectively, where the field needs to go next.

This section frames that frontier around four interlocking and still-open questions. First, whether calibrated models and their parameters actually transfer across sites, soil types, spatial scales, and longer time horizons---the empirical-basis problem. Second, which field data and process measurements, particularly for nitrogen and phosphorus biogeochemistry, remain missing to constrain and refine the routines that models encode. Third, how calibration, sensitivity analysis, and uncertainty quantification should be conducted so that parameter reliability can genuinely be assessed rather than assumed. Fourth, how model structure, model coupling, machine-learning methods, and the very instruments that supply calibration data should evolve. The synthesis moves from the empirical foundation for validation, through the data and methods still required, to the structural and instrumental advances that would make robust, transferable drainage models attainable. Throughout, the emphasis is on what is uncertain, untested, or unpublished, and on the specific hypotheses and datasets that would resolve those gaps.

\subsection{Broadening the Empirical Basis: Sites, Soils, Scales, and Time}

The most frequently articulated need is for validation datasets that span a wider range of physical conditions than any single study can supply. Models are repeatedly reported as requiring testing ``for longer periods of time and for different soil types'' \parencite{Salazar2009} and across ``different regions and soil types, using extensive field data'' \parencite{Li2026}. \textcite{Li2006} advance the most explicit programme, committing to continued testing against more observations across climate zones, soil types, and management regimes, and anticipating that empirical coefficient constants could eventually be replaced with functions of soil texture or other soil physical properties---a testable hypothesis that would convert site-specific tuning into transferable structure. \textcite{Chelil2022} cast the same concern as one of spatial robustness, arguing that testing on additional fields with variable spatial scales and soil properties is needed to confirm that parameters such as nitrate transfer velocities are genuinely transferable rather than artefacts of a single calibration. Comparable generalisation requirements attach to erosion and hydrological simulation, which demand data from several field sites and tillage conditions \parencite{Turunen2020}, and to controlled-drainage algorithms, whose effectiveness ``under different conditions is critically needed'' before higher credibility can be claimed \parencite{Negm2017}.

\subsubsection{Verification at independent sites}

Verification at independent sites is presented as an essential precondition for wider application, not an optional confirmation. Authors variously insist that model performance be verified ``at different experimental sites where observed data are available'' \parencite{Azevedo1997}, that testing at other sites underpin single-site findings \parencite{Qi2015}, and that further validation with additional field-observed data precede broader use \parencite{Kalita1998,Sadhukhan2019b}. Site-specific algorithms developed for spectral or soil-moisture applications are similarly flagged for validation and adaptation before extension to new areas \parencite{Rijal2013}, and inconsistencies that produce conflicting parameter values must be resolved before wider deployment \parencite{Dar2022}. Where models are inherently empirical, geographic transfer is envisioned only through re-parameterisation with local data on soils, geology, crops, and climate \parencite{Azevedo1997}, and through the deduction of region-specific coefficients where suitable reference values exist \parencite{FathAllah2025}. The open question is not whether such re-parameterisation is possible but how much local data it requires and whether coefficients can be predicted from measurable site attributes rather than re-fitted anew.

\subsubsection{Climatic, management, and design envelopes}

Extending validation across the climatic and management envelope is a closely related priority. Models developed under humid conditions are singled out for testing under semi-arid or arid regimes, and for improved simulation of rainfall events following prolonged dry spells \parencite{Prasher1996}---a regime in which current formulations are least constrained. Longer monitoring records are repeatedly invoked as the route to firmer conclusions: continued field measurement over ``a much longer period of time'' is needed to evaluate drainage-envelope materials such as rice husk \parencite{Ebrahimian2011}, and more detailed data over longer periods might have altered both system response and apparent model performance \parencite{Negm2017}. Continued field monitoring and modelling are cited as the means to validate reported results \parencite{Grazhdani1996}, and field verification is recommended before analytical tools are used for design or planning \parencite{Hornbuckle2012}. Design geometry adds a further, still-unresolved dimension: authors call for experiments exploring drain spacings wider than 30~m \parencite{Mathew2001} and for regional answers to how much drainage-efficiency loss to expect and which design rainstorm to adopt \parencite{Wiskow2003}. Several authors stress that combinations of measures and models must be adapted and optimised to local field conditions rather than assumed portable \parencite{MULLER1988}, and that field studies remain necessary simply to confirm that simulations hold \parencite{Shipitalo2016,Yousfi2014}. The recurring implication is that portability is a hypothesis to be tested empirically, not a property to be presumed.

\subsection{Field Data and Process Measurements Still Needed}

Beneath the demand for broader validation lies a scarcity of the process-level measurements needed to constrain the biogeochemical and hydrological routines that drainage models encode. The frontier here is as much about instrumented field campaigns and sampling design as about modelling per se.

\subsubsection{Nitrogen biogeochemistry}

Nitrogen dynamics emerge as the least well-constrained component of drainage models. \textcite{David2009} argue that systematic measurements of denitrification are required to test and refine existing models and to supply data for all major simulated nitrogen fluxes; their idealised design is a multi-year, tile-drained corn--soybean study measuring every major N flux alongside a full suite of physical measurements, with a pragmatic fallback of measuring a few key fluxes plus denitrification to benchmark modelled values. A parameterisation grounded primarily in field and laboratory measurements, denitrification foremost among them, is likewise judged necessary to improve nitrogen simulation \parencite{Salazar2009}. Within SWAT specifically, improvements to soil-nitrogen algorithms are called for, together with better parameterisation and constraint through more detailed agronomic data, and the routine evaluation and reporting of soil-N fluxes such as mineralisation and denitrification as standard practice \parencite{Ikenberry2017}. Long-term plot-scale studies developing better carbon and nitrogen estimates are proposed to improve parameterisation and to help reconcile the persistent discrepancy between plot- and subbasin-scale results, with cover-crop studies reporting long-term C and N dynamics singled out for validating long-term response simulations \parencite{Singer2011}. Better resolution of the interactions between soil-N processes and nitrogen export is needed to estimate the lag time between best-management-practice implementation and measurable reductions, alongside individual site assessment \parencite{Law2022}. Continued measurement and verification of parameters are also planned for improved subsurface-drainage modelling under heterogeneous soil nitrogen, where uncertainty prediction is flagged for future assessment \parencite{Tao2021}. Together these define an ambitious empirical programme: multi-year, flux-resolved, denitrification-centred campaigns that current datasets almost never provide.

\subsubsection{Phosphorus, sediment, and preferential flow}

Phosphorus transfer is similarly data-limited, and its dominant transport pathway---preferential flow through macropores and tiles---is precisely the one hardest to observe. Many factors in phosphorus indices require further investigation for their effect on field-to-stream transfer, and a central challenge is to demonstrate that index-based field rankings actually reflect the risk of transfer to open streams \parencite{Bechmann2007}. A risk-class approach relying on a multiple-factor index, supported by a large dataset, is advanced as a more reliable predictor of phosphorus leaching \parencite{Beauchemin2003}. Confirming suggested trends in phosphorus movement is argued to require quantitative measurement of preferential flow using tracers \parencite{Salazar2011}, and higher temporal sampling resolution or tracer studies are needed more generally to prove preferential flow and expose the processes behind observed concentration patterns \parencite{Tiemeyer2009}. Structural model deficiencies around surface-runoff generation, surface phosphorus loading, and phosphorus transport in tiles are identified as priorities for more accurate field-scale representation \parencite{Apostel2021}. Where accurate phosphorus-transport dynamics are required, automated samplers or real-time sensors are considered necessary \parencite{Dialameh2022}, and sediment behaviour along drainage ditches is proposed as a target for future measurement, aided by high-resolution topographic survey before and after major storms \parencite{Warsta2014}. The unifying gap is temporal and process resolution: the flashy, event-driven nature of phosphorus and sediment export outpaces conventional sampling and remains poorly captured by present models.

\subsubsection{Sampling design and analytical methods}

Sampling frequency and analytical methodology are recurring practical constraints on validation-data quality, and improving them is itself a research priority. Weekly sampling may be required to capture complete nitrate spikes before they are displaced below suction-cup samplers \parencite{Zotarelli2007}; more frequent sampling at the start of the growing season is advised for model validation \parencite{Kalita1998}; and important summer discharge events should be sampled whenever they occur \parencite{Tiemeyer2009}. Further methodological work is called for on the total-nitrogen analysis of aqueous samples by the Dumas method \parencite{Schindler1999}. More broadly, the difficulty and expense of collecting certain field data---such as water depth in potholes---is noted as a reason so few studies exist and a barrier to progress \parencite{Upadhyay2019}. Expanding monitoring networks is offered as part of the solution: edge-of-field networks provide the information needed to implement field-level management, and remote-sensing advances---LiDAR-refined digital elevation models, satellite-based leaf-area index, and tile-drainage detection---increase the spatially explicit data available for model inputs and calibration \parencite{Apostel2021}. Improved sampling geometry is also recommended for spatial characterisation, where adding closer points would sharpen semi-variograms and hence the determination of optimal sampling density \parencite{GALLICHAND1992}. These threads point toward denser, higher-frequency, and more spatially explicit observation as a precondition for the next generation of validation.

\subsection{Calibration, Sensitivity Analysis, and Uncertainty}

How models are calibrated, and how their parameters are interrogated, is treated as inseparable from the question of validity. This is arguably the methodological heart of the frontier: without defensible calibration and honest uncertainty accounting, broader validation datasets cannot be exploited.

\subsubsection{Calibration strategy and hydraulic inputs}

Multiobjective calibration frameworks that integrate statistical and graphical diagnostics---with calibration strategies tailored to the specific hydrological system and data, and graphical tools used routinely alongside statistical metrics---are advocated for more defensible model evaluation \parencite{Bruhn2025}. Reliable soil-hydraulic data, especially for the layer in which the drain is located or the deepest layers, is emphasised as a key calibration input; calibration is advised to account for parameters that act mainly through interaction, with parameters of negligible total effect fixed at nominal values \parencite{Wang2006}. Independent calibration of particular parameters against observed drainage discharge is recommended so that interception and storage terms are not conflated with hydraulic conductivity and drainable porosity \parencite{Henine2022}. In empirical drainage-recession settings, careful calibration of the most sensitive parameters is urged to reduce prediction error, and drainage-design variables such as depth and spacing are to be treated cautiously according to field setting \parencite{Timalsina2023}. Care is likewise urged where pedotransfer estimates substitute for measured properties: Rosetta-estimated saturated hydraulic conductivity should be used cautiously when no alternative exists, with further calibration recommended so that both water-table depth and drainage discharge are accurately simulated \parencite{Malota2022}. A common thread is that identifiability---separating parameters that genuinely act from those merely correlated with others---remains an under-resolved problem in drainage calibration.

\subsubsection{Automated and global approaches}

Automated and global approaches to calibration and sensitivity analysis are widely advocated as the means to make parameter interrogation systematic rather than ad hoc. Automatic calibration methods are called for to support robust parameter sensitivity analysis, with ensemble Kalman filtering identified as a promising option for parameter identification \parencite{Warsta2014}, and autocalibration is recommended alongside algorithms to accelerate solution of the Richards equation \parencite{Sadhukhan2019b}. Automatic calibration is noted as an increasingly suggested general practice \parencite{Singh2019b}. Global sensitivity analysis is proposed to capture the parameter interactions that local one-at-a-time methods such as LH-OAT cannot resolve \parencite{Timalsina2023}; sensitivity analysis is recommended to understand parameter influence on model performance in depth \parencite{Chelil2022}; and model sensitivity to all parameters is urged to be analysed under several flow conditions \parencite{Kacimov2000}. For revised SWAT, further studies are needed to establish suitable lower and upper bounds of the sensitivity adjustment factor, with additional work underway on winter and growing-season water and nitrogen budgets \parencite{Moriasi2013b}. At catchment scale, spatially distributed streambed conductance is proposed for reach-level calibration, and groundwater--surface-water exchange fluxes are suggested both as additional calibration targets and as a possible proxy for the drainage contribution to streamflow \parencite{Karan2021}.

\subsubsection{Uncertainty quantification}

Explicit treatment of uncertainty is a distinct and comparatively underdeveloped frontier. Consideration of uncertainty arising from parameter variability and measurement error is recommended \parencite{Alzraiee2013b}, and the role of input-data quality---encompassing both parameterisation and driving weather data---on model performance and output uncertainty is highlighted as an area for further study \parencite{Upadhyay2019}, with uncertainty prediction flagged for future assessment in nitrogen-loss modelling \parencite{Tao2021}. For index-based predictions, a large dataset is considered necessary to improve reliability \parencite{Beauchemin2003}. The recurring message is that drainage models are too often reported as point predictions when their inputs and parameters warrant explicit, propagated uncertainty bounds.

\subsection{Model Structure, Coupling, and Learning Approaches}

Beyond recalibration, authors identify structural refinements and integrative strategies as necessary for genuine progress. The frontier here spans model coupling, machine learning, and targeted repairs to the physical and analytical cores of drainage models.

\subsubsection{Coupling and data assimilation}

Coupling complementary models is a common and largely untested proposal. Linking a biogeochemical model to spatial hydrological models such as SWAT or MIKE SHE is suggested to enhance regional-scale prediction \parencite{Li2006}; coupling nitrate-transport and hydrological drainage models is planned, together with assimilation of spring-fertilisation information via variational data assimilation \parencite{Chelil2022}; and the integrated use of more than one model or approach is judged more practical than reliance on a single model \parencite{Singh2019b}. As spatial scales are refined, more fine-scale data are needed to calibrate and validate increasingly complex models, and advanced computer-learning and data-assimilation methods are advocated to exploit the growing volume of information, along with refinement of parameter estimates to smaller scales \parencite{Apostel2021}.

\subsubsection{Machine learning and its validation needs}

Machine-learning models raise their own validation requirements, which the literature is only beginning to confront. Tuning hyperparameters and incorporating more diverse datasets---potentially via systematic grid or random search---is proposed to improve drain-pipe detection \parencite{Song2021}, and artificial neural networks are noted to require training data that include extreme situations, being readily retrained as such data accrue \parencite{Yang2000}. Several structural deficiencies of process models are named directly: site characterisation must be detailed enough to simulate physical processes and truly understand the system; regression limitations may be overcome by adding independent variables and non-linear dependences; and submodels for nitrogen mineralisation, crop nitrogen uptake, and plant growth under nitrogen limitation should be examined, improved, and freed of conceptual deficiencies \parencite{Ahuja2007}. More physically based improvements to soil-nitrogen algorithms are similarly warranted \parencite{Ikenberry2017}, and additional model complexity is identified as the price of improving predicted timing of drainage during snowmelt and soil thaw \parencite{Feyereisen2007}.

\subsubsection{Physical and analytical refinements}

Refinements to the physical and analytical basis of drainage models constitute a rich set of concrete, testable proposals. Numerical models incorporating aquifer homogeneity, anisotropy, and varying bottom slopes are envisioned, with existing analytical solutions serving to verify them \parencite{Lin2025}; conversely, results from simple models are recommended for cross-checking against more general models and vice versa, collating several analytical approaches, running numerical codes such as MODFLOW, and verifying in the field \parencite{Kacimov2000}. Reanalysis using the Laplace rather than the Boussinesq equation, and extension of in-situ validation to slow-response systems, are proposed \parencite{Bouarfa2000}, while comparison of semi-analytical, fully numerical, and extended analytical solutions is reported as ongoing \parencite{Wiskow2003}. Recession-based equations are proposed for extension to spatially varying soil properties by replacing constant hydraulic conductivity and drainable porosity with functions of distance from the drain, with the effect of slope requiring further work \parencite{Cooke2001}. A three-dimensional flow model is suggested as potentially more suitable at a heterogeneous field site, with independent mathematical treatment and more controlled laboratory and field experiments warranted to make similar-media scaling approaches more robust \parencite{Mohanty1997}. Solute mixing is proposed to be represented with distinct mixing-layer depths and partition coefficients for the mixing and outflow stages, the exact nature of the process remaining to be investigated \parencite{Shalit1996}. Additional targeted refinements include reconsidering the assumption of constant deep percolation and the treatment of shallow water tables persisting within 0.3~m of the surface \parencite{Kolars2019}, further study of spacing and depth through experiment and steady-state analytic--numerical simulation \parencite{Saptomo2021}, and concurrent comparison of outflow and water-table recessions observed at a single site \parencite{VANDELEUR1959}. Strengthening index-system construction and the evaluation of calculation reliability---supported by more samples and additional indicators such as soil thickness, grain size, composition, density, effective porosity, and permeability---is urged to enable deeper mechanistic development \parencite{Hou2018}, and building a dedicated simulation model to extend experimental findings is likewise planned \parencite{Tao2019b}.

\subsection{Instrumentation and Measurement-Method Validation}

Because model validation is only as sound as the data feeding it, a substantial share of the frontier concerns the instruments and equations used to measure drainage flows and concentrations. This is an easily overlooked source of error: uncalibrated flow structures and drifting sensors can masquerade as model inadequacy.

\subsubsection{Flow-structure and sensor calibration}

In-house calibration of flow-measurement structures is recommended repeatedly. Weir calibration is advised for individual applications wherever possible \parencite{Christianson2019}; in-house calibrations representing field flow conditions are urged for accurate subsurface-discharge estimation, with an established equation recommended for one control-structure size and in-house calibration for others \parencite{Katuwal2024}; and individual calibration is likewise deemed necessary for weirs generally \parencite{Napierala2023}. Development of a stage--discharge equation before drainage monitoring is recommended for cylindrical control structures, with an ultrasonic sensor under investigation for compatibility with commercial structures \parencite{BinShokrana2021}. Post-hoc sensor calibration against collected samples, and grab-sample laboratory verification to provide test-specific calibration of bromide sensors during tracer tests, are recommended to improve logged-value accuracy \parencite{deOliveira2023b}.

\subsubsection{Emerging measurement technologies}

Emerging measurement technologies carry explicit validation requirements before they can be trusted in the field. A computer-vision flow-measurement approach requires more data to verify robustness in real-world environments and testing under more variable conditions, with model modifications---such as incorporating the Manning--Strickler equation to compensate for uneven horizontal initial velocities---and attention to data storage, transmission, and reduction for long-term deployment \parencite{Young2023}. For high-precision topographic survey under less-than-ideal real-time-kinematic conditions, hybrid RTK--rotating-laser systems or interpolated total-station surfaces are offered as alternatives \parencite{Freeland2014}. Sensor and device design is also flagged for refinement, including adaptation of a basic design for other target ranges and improvement of the coefficient of variation for mass-produced units \parencite{Enamorado2007}, and examination of the humidity-stability effect on sensible-heat-flux estimates from large-aperture scintillometry \parencite{Zhang2010}.

\subsubsection{Hydraulic-loss characterisation}

Hydraulic-loss measurement in drainage conduits remains conspicuously incomplete, limiting the physical realism of design equations. Further work is needed on head loss in corrugated-pipe junctions and manholes under free-surface flow, with the possibility of discharge-specific head-loss coefficient relationships noted \parencite{Bayat2011}; on friction energy loss, flow velocities, detailed free-surface elevations, and transitional cavity-flow conditions \parencite{Nédélec2008}; on the influence of declining-bend slope on air discharge across several distortion heights \parencite{Gurklys2008}; and on modifying existing formulae to apply an appropriate Manning coefficient for design \parencite{Kouchakzadeh2011}. Strain-gauge experiments are identified as necessary to generate input data characterising the non-linear behaviour of single-wall corrugated HDPE pipe \parencite{Gaj2021}.

\subsection{Design Optimisation, Feasibility, and Implementation}

A final strand of the frontier extends validation from hydrological and biogeochemical performance to design optimisation, economic feasibility, and the institutional context of implementation---dimensions in which field-scale evidence is thinnest.

\subsubsection{Optimisation and feasibility}

Optimal, least-cost design methodologies are sought that allow all alternative designs to be compared \parencite{Bakour2021}, and more optimal design approaches---such as two-column phosphorus-sorption-media configurations that reduce the required media, potentially supported by governmental conservation incentives---are proposed for edge-of-field treatment systems \parencite{Hauda2020}. Models are also envisioned as tools for exploring amended drain layouts and altered irrigation schedules \parencite{Alzraiee2013b}. Feasibility studies frame several technologies as unproven at field scale: numerous technical and economic problems---system hydraulics, operating procedures, regenerant-waste disposal, and the physical design and cost of a field-scale unit---must be solved before ion-exchange nitrate removal can be implemented \parencite{MAGETTE1990}, and socio-economic aspects and construction adequacy must be analysed alongside engineering considerations for drainage structures \parencite{Bozic2014}. At the institutional level, financiers and governments are urged to recognise capacity building as a complex, lengthy process warranting long-term investment, with a knowledge-creation framework offered to guide strategy \parencite{Ritzema2008}.

\subsubsection{Statistical and monitoring-design refinements}

Several statistical and monitoring-design refinements round out the agenda for field validation. For regional soil-property prediction, weighting prediction times through exponential or logarithmic transformation, more objective assembly of model libraries, bagging of hillclimb datasets, and---at farm and field scale---proximal sensors such as ground-penetrating radar and electromagnetic induction are proposed for future study \parencite{Moller2018}. Analyses of within-field variation are advanced through additional lag parameters to capture response times exceeding one measurement interval, random monitoring-well effects where multiple wells lie at similar distances from drains, comparison of nearby fields under different treatments, and alternative physically based numerical models such as SWAP, HYDRUS, SUTRA, or MODFLOW \parencite{vandeCraats2025}. Finally, in-situ field validation planned or achieved but not yet fully reported \parencite{Hartani2001}, and follow-up work on solute transport during preferential flow and on spatial-variability treatment via similar-media scaling \parencite{Mohanty1997}, signal that much of the empirical testing the field regards as essential remains to be completed and published. Coupled with agreement that large-scale hydrologic applications and the simulation of additional, untested drain spacings are natural next steps \parencite{Talukolaee2022}, these open threads define a validation programme that is broad in scale, long in duration, and rich in the process measurements that current datasets conspicuously lack.

\subsection{Synthesis: A Validation Programme for the Field}

The gaps enumerated above converge on a coherent, if demanding, forward agenda. Empirically, the field needs multi-site, multi-season datasets that deliberately span soil types, climates, management regimes, and design geometries, so that transferability can be tested rather than assumed and empirical coefficients can be reformulated as functions of measurable site properties \parencite{Li2006,Chelil2022}. Methodologically, it needs automated and global calibration coupled with explicit, propagated uncertainty, so that broader datasets translate into demonstrably reliable parameters rather than better-fitted but still fragile ones \parencite{Warsta2014,Timalsina2023,Alzraiee2013b}. In terms of process understanding, the scarcity of denitrification and preferential-flow measurements marks nitrogen and phosphorus biogeochemistry as the routines most in need of new field and laboratory data \parencite{David2009,Salazar2011}. Structurally, model coupling, data assimilation, and machine learning offer routes to regional-scale and data-rich prediction, but each imports its own validation obligations \parencite{Apostel2021,Song2021}. And underpinning all of these, the instruments and equations that supply calibration data---flow structures, sensors, and hydraulic-loss formulae---require their own systematic validation before the data they yield can be trusted \parencite{Christianson2019,Bayat2011}. The consistent message of this literature is that the next decade of progress depends less on new equations than on the sustained, well-instrumented, and openly reported field campaigns needed to test the equations already in hand.
